BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-52853945971574@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260630T022433Z
DTSTART:20261118T163000Z
DTEND:20261118T220000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Suicide Assessment and Treatment: An Update for Profession
 als
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/suicide-assessment-and-treatment-an-u
 pdate-for-professionals
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/52853945971574.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/suic
 ide-assessment-and-treatment-an-update-for-professionals\n報名/票券: h
 ttps://stanford.cloud-cme.com/course/courseoverview?P=0&EID=57285\n\nOverv
 iew8:30 AM (PST) - 2:00 PM (PST) | 11:30 AM (EST) - 5:00 PM (EST)\n\nWith 
 suicide rates increasing by 30% in the last 2 decades\, suicide remains a 
 pressing public health challenge. This course provides an overview of key 
 topics in suicide assessment\, prevention\, and treatment that is relevant
  to all treatment settings and clinical disciplines. This course will cove
 r assessment factors such as family history\, past trauma\, social isolati
 on\, and support systems and cultural/religious beliefs that stigmatize me
 ntal health or suicide. Prevention topics addressed will cover recognizing
  warning signs\, responding to crises\, and connecting individuals to appr
 opriate resources and enhancing social support networks and community resi
 lience. Putting concepts into practice\, the course will also cover treatm
 ent methods such as psychotherapy\, medication\, hospitalization for acute
  risk or severe mental health crises\, collaborative care models\, follow-
 up care and ongoing monitoring.\n\nRegistrationNo CE Credit - you will not
  be able to obtain credit: Free\nCE Credit: $65\n\nFor those who register\
 , they will receive a link to the 247 page comprehensive and recently upda
 ted online resource entitled Current Status of Suicide-Focused Assessment 
 and Treatment - click here to see an overview.\n\nCreditsAMA PRA Category 
 1 Credits™ (5.00 hours)\, ANCC Contact Hours (5.00 hours)\, APA Continui
 ng Education credits (5.00 hours)\, ASWB Continuing Education (ACE) credit
 s (5.00 hours)\, Non-Physician Participation
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-52941068362323@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260923T234013Z
DTSTART:20261210T200000Z
DTEND:20261210T210000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Lunchtime Curator Talk | Solid Pictures
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center\, Pigott Family Gallery (142)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-lunchtime-curator-talk-solid-
 pictures
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/52941068362323.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/copy
 -of-lunchtime-curator-talk-solid-pictures\n報名/票券: https://thethird
 place.is/event/solidpictures1210\n\n​Join Patrick R. Crowley\, Associate
  Curator of European Art\, on this special highlights tour of Solid Pictur
 es.\n\nBefore 3D printing\, there was photosculpture. Curated by Patrick R
 . Crowley\, Associate Curator of European Art\, Solid Pictures is the firs
 t major exhibition to explore these largely overlooked objects first conce
 ived in 1859 by the twenty-nine-year-old French artist and inventor Franç
 ois Willème. Employing projection technologies and mechanical instruments
 \, Willème and his team of operators translated photographic negatives in
 to solid figures across a variety of media\, aiming to create affordable p
 ortrait sculpture for the burgeoning middle class of Second Empire France.
  Though commercially unsuccessful\, these experiments laid the groundwork 
 for the scanning\, machining\, and printing technologies ubiquitous today.
  Bringing together these rare artifacts\, the exhibition opens up a little
 -known chapter of history to reveal the roots and richness of our contempo
 rary world. An accompanying catalogue features four newly commissioned ess
 ays accompanied by previously unpublished documentation\, deepening our un
 derstanding of this overlooked chapter in the history of art and technolog
 y.\n\nRSVP here.\n\n________\n\nParking\n\nPaid visitor parking is availab
 le along Lomita Drive as well as on the first floor of the Roth Way Garage
  Structure\, located at the corner of Campus Drive West and Roth Way at 34
 5 Campus Drive\, Stanford\, CA 94305. From the Palo Alto Caltrain station\
 , the Cantor 
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53251196125393@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261021T221217Z
DTSTART:20261120T013000Z
DTEND:20261120T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Holly Herndon & Mat Dryhurst: Protocol Art
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/studio-lecture-series-holly-herndon-m
 at-dryhurst
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53251196125393.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/stud
 io-lecture-series-holly-herndon-mat-dryhurst\n報名/票券: https://holly
 herndonxmatdryhurst.eventbrite.com\n\nMade possible by the Millicent Green
 well Clapp Studio Art Series Fund\, this series invites an artist each qua
 rter for a two-day visit consisting of studio critiques with our MFA stude
 nts and an evening public lecture. This series continues to provide a dyna
 mic platform for artists to engage with the Stanford community\, fostering
  critical dialogue and creative exploration.\n\nProtocol Art\n\nIn this le
 cture\, Herndon and Dryhurst discuss projects from their expansive practic
 e and their concept of Protocol Art\, an approach that treats the creation
  of and intervention in the systems\, protocols and rules shaping cultural
  production as an artistic medium.\n\nHolly Herndon and Mathew Dryhurst ar
 e Berlin-based artists renowned for their pioneering work in music\, machi
 ne learning\, and governance. Their practice creates precedent-setting pro
 jects where technical systems are artworks unto themselves\, operating at 
 the intersection of art\, technology\, and infrastructure — work they de
 scribe as Protocol Art: interventions at the level of systems and protocol
 s rather than objects\, arguing for agency at the infrastructure layer.\n\
 nTogether they run Herndon Dryhurst Studio\, and have co-produced critical
 ly acclaimed albums including Platform (4AD/RVNG 2015) and PROTO (4AD/2019
 )\, and developed Holly+\, a protocol for shared ownership and licensing o
 f an artist's identity and intellectual property.\n\nIn 2022\, they co-fou
 nded Spawning\, an organization that attempted to build a web-scale data p
 ermissions p
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53286213436273@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260628T204045Z
DTSTART:20261116T191500Z
DTEND:20261116T201500Z
SUMMARY:[現場] November Museum Minis: Storytime at the Anderson
LOCATION:Anderson Collection\, The Denning Family Resource Center 
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/November-museum-minis-storytime-at-th
 e-anderson
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53286213436273.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/Nove
 mber-museum-minis-storytime-at-the-anderson\n\nMuseum Minis is a welcoming
  monthly storytime at the Anderson Collection for children ages 0–5 and 
 their caregivers. Each session features two engaging\, age-appropriate boo
 ks inspired by the essential elements of art—line\, color\, shape\, and 
 texture—introducing young children to these ideas and connecting them to
  artworks in the museum’s collection. Together\, families are invited to
  look closely\, notice details\, and experience the museum environment in 
 an accessible\, joyful way. The Anderson Collection is home to works by ar
 tists such as Jackson Pollock\, Mark Rothko\, Joan Mitchell\, and Nick Cav
 e. The morning concludes with a light snack and time to connect.\n\n \n\nO
 ur storyteller\, Maria\, is a Visitor Experience Assistant and Museum Enga
 gement Guide at the Anderson Collection. She brings a deep love of art\, s
 torytelling\, and community to her work\, and believes that every artwork 
 — and every person — holds a story worth sharing. Outside the museum\,
  she enjoys prioritizing wellness and slowing down with a good cup of tea.
 \n\nNo RSVP necessary\, walk-ins welcome!
END:VEVENT
