UID:stanford-53497842526080@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T114809Z
DTSTART:20261111T200000Z
DTEND:20261111T210000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Children and Childhood in Colonial Senegal - Kelly Duke Br
 yant
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/children-and-childhood-in-colonial-se
 negal-kelly-duke-bryant
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53497842526080.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/chil
 dren-and-childhood-in-colonial-senegal-kelly-duke-bryant\n報名/票券: h
 ttps://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_r8SPVFeWQG67NsidVpix1A\n\nHow 
 did French colonial institutions seek to define\, regulate\, and ‘‘pro
 tect’’ childhood in Senegal\, and how did children respond? \n\nIn thi
 s talk based on her recent book\, Negotiating Childhood: French Colonialis
 m and African Children in Senegal\, 1848–1940\, Kelly Duke Bryant explor
 es the lived realities of African children across courts\, schools\, stree
 ts\, reformatories\, vaccine clinics\, and other sites of colonial interve
 ntion. Rather than treating children as passive subjects\, Bryant illumina
 tes the ways children limited or negotiated colonial reforms and how the c
 olonial state’s reliance on cooperation produced unexpected forms of res
 istance and accommodation. The talk highlights long-term consequences of c
 olonial governance\, such as the privileging of chronological age and the 
 centrality of official documentation\, that continue to shape Senegalese c
 hildhoods.\n\nSpeaker Details\n\nKelly Duke Bryant is chair of the History
  Department at Rowan University\, where she has taught since 2009. She ear
 ned her MA in history from the University of Wisconsin and her Ph.D.\, als
 o in history\, from Johns Hopkins University. A specialist in the history 
 of West Africa\, she has published scholarly articles and book chapters on
  the history of education\, childhood\, and youth in colonial Senegal. She
  is the author of two books\, Education as Politics: Colonial Schooling an
 d Political Debate in Senegal\, 1850s-1914 (University of Wisconsin Press\
 , 2015) and Negotiating Childhood: French Colonialism and
END:VEVENT
