SUMMARY:[現場] Anatomical Gift Program Donor Memorial Service
LOCATION:Memorial Church
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anatomical-gift-program-donor-memoria
 l-service
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53630642907719.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/anat
 omical-gift-program-donor-memorial-service\n報名/票券: https://stanfor
 duniversity.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_4Zxiwd4itU47Szc\n\nWe wish to expres
 s our deep appreciation for the invaluable contributions each donor has ma
 de to our program at Stanford University. Their generosity has left an ind
 elible mark\, touching the lives of countless individuals\, and we come to
 gether annually to honor this profound impact through a special memorial s
 ervice.\n\nIt is with utmost sincerity that we extend a heartfelt invitati
 on to the families of those donors who entrusted their loved ones to our c
 are between November 2025 and November 2026 to join us for this significan
 t occasion. We recognize the profound significance of this occasion and wa
 rmly welcome you to join us as we gather to honor and cherish the memory o
 f the remarkable individuals who have contributed to our program through t
 heir selfless gift.\n\nAdditional information regarding logistics and the 
 event's specific details will be communicated to you at a later date. For 
 those unable to attend in person\, a recording of the service will be made
  available for viewing following the event. \n\n \n\nThis event will begin
  at 6pm and doors will open at 5:30pm.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53691925115269@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T205424Z
DTSTART:20261105T200000Z
DTEND:20261105T210000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Documentary Objectives: Filming Africa from Colonialism to
  Independence - Rachel Gabara
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/documentary-objectives-filming-africa
 -from-colonialism-to-independence-rachel-gabara
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53691925115269.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/docu
 mentary-objectives-filming-africa-from-colonialism-to-independence-rachel-
 gabara\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/DND3whcsRUX6fDyAA\n\nSession Desc
 ription:\n\nFor well over a century\, filmmakers have been shooting docume
 ntaries in West and Central Africa\, a region that included the colonies o
 f French West and Equatorial Africa and now encompasses fourteen nations a
 nd nearly 200 million people. These films played a crucial role in the dev
 elopment of both European and African cinemas yet have been largely ignore
 d by scholars. In the wake of fifty years of French colonial documentary i
 n sub-Saharan Africa- propaganda-infused travel\, hunting\, expedition\, a
 nd ethnographic films- African directors reclaimed their cinematic image b
 y challenging outsider claims to authenticity and developing new models fo
 r nonfiction. I will highlight the nearly forgotten innovations of the dec
 ades following independence to demonstrate how\, in a complex network of i
 mages and languages and across a dynamic range of modes\, African document
 arists remade a global art form rooted in oppression\, exoticization\, and
  a simplistic conception of filmic realism.\n\nSpeaker Bio: \n\nRachel Gab
 ara is the Nancy Gillespie Brinning Professor in French at the University 
 of Georgia\, where her teaching focuses on nonfiction and historical ficti
 on from Europe and sub-Saharan Africa. In addition to Documentary Objectiv
 es\, published earlier this year by Indiana University Press\, Gabara’s 
 recent articles address early African film criticism (Global Movie Magazin
 e Networks\, University of California Press)\, cinephilia and modernism in
  African cinema of the late 19
END:VEVENT
