UID:stanford-53745967573694@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T164859Z
DTSTART:20261120T023000Z
DTEND:20261120T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Elamite Art Through the Lens of Archaeological Evidence
LOCATION:In person at Stanford
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/elamite-art-through-the-lens-of-archa
 eological-evidence
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53745967573694.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/elam
 ite-art-through-the-lens-of-archaeological-evidence\n報名/票券: https:
 //forms.gle/XFeAWe3ue3Co2h4X7\n\nEvent is in Persian.\n\nProfessor Bahram 
 Ghadiri examines Elamite art as a major body of archaeological evidence fo
 r understanding the cultural\, political\, and religious development of so
 uthwestern Iran and the broader Iranian Plateau. Rather than approaching E
 lamite art primarily as a collection of isolated aesthetic objects\, the p
 resentation situates artistic production within its historical\, geographi
 cal\, and social contexts.\n\nThe lecture begins with an overview of the e
 mergence of Elamite political formations and their changing territorial ex
 tent from the late fourth millennium to the first millennium BCE. It consi
 ders the relationships among important centers such as Susa\, Anshan\, Haf
 t Tappeh\, Chogha Zanbil\, and the highland regions associated with Elamit
 e cultural traditions.\n\nThe second section addresses the principal metho
 dological challenges involved in interpreting Elamite art. Particular atte
 ntion is given to archaeological context\, chronology\, iconography\, insc
 riptions\, patterns of cultural exchange\, and the limitations created by 
 incomplete or displaced evidence. The lecture also considers how earlier s
 cholarly frameworks have influenced modern interpretations of Elamite arti
 stic identity.\n\nThe final section examines selected examples of monument
 al architecture\, rock reliefs\, seals\, sculpture\, metalwork\, ceramics\
 , and representations of music and ritual. These materials illuminate conc
 epts of kingship\, religious practice\, social organization\, collective m
 emory\, and
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53808344432557@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T120338Z
DTSTART:20261104T230000Z
DTEND:20261105T012000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Digital Mirrors of Materiality
LOCATION:Anderson Collection\, Denning Family Resource Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/digital-mirrors-of-materiality
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53808344432557.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/digi
 tal-mirrors-of-materiality\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d
 /e/1FAIpQLSf9oEQbuYA6Q3OdZVyqOpz8lMLeu97FIc3o0pjMiFq3_rl_fQ/viewform?usp=h
 eader\n\npart of *Defying Boundaries: MacArthur Fellows at 45\n\nwith Kath
 erine Behar\, Nick Benson*\, Hideo Mabuchi*\, and Camille Utterback*\nMode
 rated by Vanessa Chang\n\nNovember 4\, 3:00-5:20PM\n\nDenning Family Resou
 rce Center\, Anderson Collection\n\nReception to follow\n5:30-6:30PM at th
 e McMurtry Building Courtyard\n\nWe are all in some way navigating widespr
 ead futureshock over paradigm-breaking growth in the power of computation 
 and robotics. Stepping back from the most expansive societal stakes to see
 k grounding at personal scale\, expressive making offers a rich context wi
 thin which to reflect upon what craft does for the maker\, and what is los
 t or gained when we augment or supplant embodied engagement with digital t
 echnologies. If our goal is not just to produce but to make things as a wa
 y of being at home in the world\, what could we possibly want from the lev
 erage of fabulous machines? Can traditional craft assimilate the expansive
 ness of the digital? How does this historical moment call for us to affirm
  the value of practicing heirloom modes of creativity?\n\n\nLearn more and
  RSVP\n****\n\nThis event made possible by John D. and Catherine T. MacArt
 hur Foundation \, Funding Good Chaos\, Stanford Arts Institute (SAI)\, the
  Department of Art and Art History  and the Office of the Vice President f
 or the Arts.
END:VEVENT
