BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53852108956001@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261029T181305Z
DTSTART:20261112T013000Z
DTEND:20261112T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] BOOK LAUNCH: Literature and Emergency Law in Kashmir and N
 orthern Ireland
LOCATION:Building 80\, Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-launch-literature-and-emergency-
 law-in-kashmir-and-northern-ireland
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53852108956001.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/book
 -launch-literature-and-emergency-law-in-kashmir-and-northern-ireland\n報
 名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdQah3_uTR1wJVo42_g9N
 lrI24aytDewDVEZUSI91E2EPRHxg/viewform\n\nThe Department of African and Afr
 ican American Studies (DAAAS) in collaboration with the Center for South A
 sia (CSA) cordially invite the Stanford Community for a special book launc
 h!\n\nWe are excited to celebrate the publication of a new book by our vis
 iting postdoctoral scholar\, Danny Shanhan\, Ph.D titled\, Literature and 
 Emergency Law in Kashmir and Northern Ireland.\n\nThis book is the first s
 ubstantial study of emergency law's relationship to literature in either t
 he Northern Irish or the Kashmiri conflict and develops an original legal 
 history that links these laws to a shared British colonial root. Engaging 
 a wide range of fiction\, poetry and film\, from canonical poets Seamus He
 aney and Agha Shahid Ali to contemporary works by Anna Burns\, Mirza Wahee
 d\, and Bollywood filmmaker Vishal Bhardwaj\, the book examines the litera
 ry response to the suspension of normative legal rights such as habeas cor
 pus\, the right to silence and even\, in Kashmir\, the right to life. By b
 ringing these literary cultures together for the first time\, it develops 
 a literary and legal history that reveals common roots in colonial-era jur
 isprudence. The book re-situates these corpora\, often isolated in scholar
 ly work\, as important parts of the global\, material legacies of colonial
 ism.\n\nJoin us for an evening of conversation\, community and celebration
  and Danny shares his work. This will be followed by a discussion and Q&A 
 with Roanne Kantor\, Assistant Professor of English.\n\nRefreshments will 
 be provided.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53853306172764@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260922T010602Z
DTSTART:20261204T200000Z
DTEND:20261204T200000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Who’s Afraid of the East Romans?
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/whos-afraid-of-the-east-romans
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53853306172764.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/whos
 -afraid-of-the-east-romans\n\nRecent years have seen a push to recognize t
 he east Roman state and its people for what and who they said they were 
 — Romans — and not to hide that reality under the invented term “Byz
 antium\,” which has always encoded negative stereotypes. In this talk\, 
 professor Kaldellis will discuss the ideologies and structural interests t
 hat have resisted acknowledging a Roman people in the east\, ranging from 
 western Eurocentrism to modern Greek nationalism. “Rome" has long been a
  foundational matrix for thinking about western identities and states: wha
 t would it look like if it made room to accommodate its eastern branch on 
 an equal basis?\n\nRSVP here.\n\nAnthony Kaldellis is a professor of Class
 ics at the University of Chicago. He has published widely on the history\,
  culture\, and literature of Byzantium\, ranging from the fourth to the fi
 fteenth centuries. His works include a new\, comprehensive history of the 
 eastern Roman empire: The New Roman Empire: A History of Byzantium (Oxford
 \, 2023). Most recently he has published a new history of the fall of Cons
 tantinople in 1453 and a study of western perceptions of Byzantium from la
 te antiquity to World War II (Phantom Byzantium). He has translated mediev
 al Greek authors\, including historians such as Prokopios\, Attaleiates\, 
 and Laonikos Chalkokondyles\, and is the host of the academic podcast “B
 yzantium & Friends\,” going strong since 2019.
END:VEVENT
