UID:stanford-53862726309410@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260903T060649Z
DTSTART:20270115T010000Z
DTEND:20270115T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Eugene Wang: "Biocosmic Art\; or How to Capture a Painter
 ’s Brain?"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/eugene-wang-biocosmic-art-or-how-to-c
 apture-a-painters-brain
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53862726309410.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/euge
 ne-wang-biocosmic-art-or-how-to-capture-a-painters-brain\n\nAbstract:\n\nW
 hen medical imaging mapped the neural deterioration of the legendary Hong 
 Kong ink painter Irene Chou (1924-2011)\, she looked at her own brain X-ra
 ys and experienced a startling revelation: “I saw vital images of tree b
 ranches… that is life—my life.” In this lecture\, Eugene Wang (Harva
 rd University) reconsiders Chou’s art through the converging lenses of p
 lant sensience\, systems biology\, and Buddhist-cum-Daoist meditative prac
 tice\, revealing her paintings not as abstract landscapes but as experimen
 ts in biocosmic imagination. From the refuge of her father’s photographi
 c darkroom in Shanghai to her solitary\, obsessive sketches of “ten thou
 sand trees” in Hong Kong’s dense woods\, Chou turned ink into a means 
 of self-preservation: thousands of rhythmic parallel lines became protecti
 ve membranes\, holding an endangered inner world against chaos. Bringing C
 harles Darwin’s studies of plant movement\, Herbert Read’s modernist a
 esthetics\, medical imaging\, and the alchemical cosmology of breathing ex
 ercise into dialogue\, Wang shows how Chou transformed illness and vulnera
 bility into paintings of biocosmic imaginative reach—works that move fro
 m capillary and cell to vortex and galaxy\, forging a radiant visual lexic
 on of resilience\, renewal\, and cosmic liberation. \n\nBio:\n\nEugene Y. 
 Wang is the Abby Aldrich Rockefeller Professor of Asian Art at Harvard Uni
 versity. A Guggenheim Fellow and art-history editor of the Encyclopedia of
  Buddhism\, he is the author of Shaping the Lotus Su
END:VEVENT
