UID:stanford-53862757496054@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260904T105640Z
DTSTART:20270129T010000Z
DTEND:20270129T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Anning Jing: "Borobudur and a Tantric Buddhist Maṇḍala
  Associated with Vajrabodhi (671–741)"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anning-jing-borobudur-and-a-tantric-b
 uddhist-maala-associated-with-vajrabodhi-671-741
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53862757496054.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/anni
 ng-jing-borobudur-and-a-tantric-buddhist-maala-associated-with-vajrabodhi-
 671-741\n\nAbstract:\n\nThis talk reexamines Borobudur through the transmi
 ssion of Esoteric Buddhist teachings associated with Indian Tantric Buddhi
 st master Vajrabodhi (671–741). It argues that Vajrabodhi’s activities
  in Java and China\, his transmission of Tantric teachings to Amoghavajra\
 , and the subsequent initiation of the Javanese monk Bianhong into the Gar
 bhadhātu Maṇḍala by Amoghavajra’s disciple Huiguo created the histo
 rical and doctrinal framework for Borobudur’s construction. A three-dime
 nsional Buddhist maṇḍala known as the shi 式 or “secret disc”\, d
 escribed in a Tang Buddhist text attributed to Vajrabodhi\, closely parall
 els Borobudur’s architectural form\, cosmological model\, iconographic p
 rogram of the Five Jina Buddhas\, and solar symbolism. It proposes that Bo
 robudur was conceived as a monumental celestial maṇḍala of Mahāvairoc
 ana\, shaped by Vajrabodhi’s Esoteric Buddhist lineage and its cosmologi
 cal vision. Vajrabodhi’s shi\, however\, was rooted in the traditional C
 hinese shi\, whose origins can be traced to the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture
  of southeastern China.\n\nBio:\n\nAnning Jing received his MA in Art Hist
 ory from Ohio State University in 1989 and PhD in Art History from Princet
 on University in 1993. He has been teaching Asian art at Michigan State Un
 iversity since 1995. His main publications include The Water God's Temple 
 of the Guangsheng Monastery: Cosmic Function of Art\, Ritual\, and Theater
  (Leiden: Brill\, 2002)\, Yuandai biha “Shenxianfuhui tu” (The Yuan Mu
 rals “Assembly o
END:VEVENT
