SUMMARY:[現場] Book Event with Roland Betancourt and T.L. Taylor\, "Disne
 yland and the Rise of Automation"
LOCATION:Green Library\, Bing Wing\, 122
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-event-with-roland-betancourt-and
 -tl-taylor-disneyland-and-the-rise-of-automation
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53949492427777.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/book
 -event-with-roland-betancourt-and-tl-taylor-disneyland-and-the-rise-of-aut
 omation\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdIh4Oe5g
 c56WhNKyXZ0kvVtQiTgrdns8_WbXFBcnrHAQGhDQ/viewform?usp=publish-editor\n\nPl
 ease join the Silicon Valley Archives in welcoming Roland Betancourt and T
 .L. Taylor as they discuss Betancourt's latest book\, Disneyland and Rise 
 of Automation\, on December 2\, 2026\, at 4:30 p.m. \n\n \n\nDisneyland an
 d the Rise of Automation argues that theme park rides are factory assembly
  lines in disguise\, ones that manufacture subjects rather than products. 
 The book traces the technological systems that made this possible\, from l
 imit switches and relay racks to programmable logic controllers and magnet
 ic tape\, revealing how innovations developed for wartime manufacturing an
 d Cold War industry found new life in the Happiest Place on Earth. It appl
 ies insights from the history of technology to problems shared by historia
 ns\, media scholars\, and critics of visual culture\, such as the aestheti
 cs of automation and the politics of labor.\n\n  \n\nRoland Betancourt is 
 a Chancellor’s Professor at the University of California\, Irvine. He is
  currently the Andrew W. Mellon Professor at the Center for Advanced Study
  in the Visual Arts at the National Gallery of Art. A 2023 Guggenheim Fell
 ow\, he has published books on Byzantine visual culture\, gender\, and sex
 uality\, including the prize-winning Byzantine Intersectionality (Princeto
 n\, 2020).\n\n \n\nT.L. Taylor is Professor of Comparative Media Studies a
 t MITand affiliate faculty member in Anthropology and Women’s and Gender
  Studies. She is a qualitative sociologist who has focused on the interrel
 ations between culture and technology in online en
END:VEVENT
