BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53976866896468@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T164655Z
DTSTART:20261119T013000Z
DTEND:20261119T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Disappearance of Enforced Disappearances in Kashmir: M
 ilitarization\, Mourning\, and the Production of Normalcy
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-disappearance-of-enforced-disappe
 arances-in-kashmir-militarization-mourning-and-the-production-of-normalcy-
 9170
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53976866896468.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 disappearance-of-enforced-disappearances-in-kashmir-militarization-mournin
 g-and-the-production-of-normalcy-9170\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/LF
 swcLeYLkkXfHJfA\n\nThis event is co-sponsored by the Center for South Asia
  \, the Abbasi Program in Islamic Studies \, and the Department of Anthrop
 ology.\n\nAbout the speaker\nAther Zia\, PhD\, is a political anthropologi
 st\, poet\, short fiction writer\, and columnist. She is a Professor of An
 thropology and Gender Studies at the University of Northern Colorado\, Gre
 eley. She is the author of Resisting Disappearances: Military Occupation a
 nd Women’s Activism in Kashmir (University of Washington Press\, 2019\; 
 Zubaan Books South Asia edition\, 2020). She is the co-editor of Can You H
 ear Kashmiri Women Speak? (Women Unlimited\, 2020)\, Resisting Occupation 
 in Kashmir (University of Pennsylvania Press\, 2018)\, and A Desolation Ca
 lled Peace (HarperCollins\, 2019). Her recent poetry collection In Kashmir
 : Writing Under Occupation was published by Agitate Collective. She is the
  founder-editor of Kashmir Lit\, and co-founder of the Critical Kashmir St
 udies Collective. Ather is also a former co-editor of Cultural Anthropolog
 y (2022–2025) and Associate Editor of the Encyclopedia of Women & Islami
 c Cultures.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53977038029919@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T121201Z
DTSTART:20261106T003000Z
DTEND:20261106T020000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Fate of the Social in Modern Life: American Social Sci
 entists and Japan as Archive
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-fate-of-the-social-in-modern-life
 -american-social-scientists-and-japan-as-archive
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53977038029919.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 fate-of-the-social-in-modern-life-american-social-scientists-and-japan-as-
 archive\n報名/票券: https://luma.com/5ach1gu9\n\nIn the decades follow
 ing World War II\, American social scientists turned to the study of Japan
 ese society as an emerging ally and fellow capitalist democracy. At a time
  when influential theories of modernization predicted the decline of kinsh
 ip\, community\, hierarchy\, and collective obligation\, Japan presented a
  puzzle. It was an advanced industrial and capitalist society in which vil
 lage\, family\, corporate welfare\, and moral persuasion in public institu
 tions played powerful roles in organizing everyday life.\n\n​This talk e
 xamines how postwar scholars treated Japan not simply as an object of stud
 y but as a site for thinking through a larger problem in modern social tho
 ught: the relationship between individual freedom and social solidarity. D
 rawing on chapters from a larger book project\, I revisit three influentia
 l studies of Japan: Ruth Benedict's The Chrysanthemum and the Sword (1946)
 \, Ezra Vogel's Japan's New Middle Class (1963)\, and Thomas Rohlen's For 
 Harmony and Strength (1974). I argue that these works are best understood 
 not only as interpretations of Japan but also as reflections on what I cal
 l the "social question" in postwar thought: how modern societies cultivate
  attachment\, obligation\, welfare\, and collective purpose while preservi
 ng democratic freedoms and individual autonomy. By placing these texts in 
 conversation\, the talk suggests that postwar Japan functioned as a distin
 ctive archive for thinking about enduring questions of social reproduction
  and social solida
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53977460472650@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T121201Z
DTSTART:20261104T180000Z
DTEND:20261104T190000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] CSRE PhD Minor Info Session
LOCATION:Building 360\, CCSRE Conference Room\, Room 361J
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/csre-phd-minor-info-session-3203
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53977460472650.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/csre
 -phd-minor-info-session-3203\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms
 /d/e/1FAIpQLSfbwzLrPW4WQIJwOfCtNP6reVLBJYtLPEk-gaElAg5nqVLQ9w/viewform?usp
 =sharing&ouid=106307572817847635960\n\nAre you interested in pursuing a gr
 aduate certificate in race and ethnic studies? \n\nFind out more about the
  PhD Minor in Comparative Studies in Race and Ethnicity (CSRE)! \n\nJoin u
 s over pastries and coffee/tea to learn more about coursework\, degree req
 uirements\, the declaration process\, and the PhD Minor community. Program
  leadership and current PhD Minors will be on hand to answer all your ques
 tions. \n\nRSVP HERE!!!\n\nFeel free to reach out to CCSRE Executive Direc
 tor\, Annie Atura\, at atura@stanford.edu with any questions about the eve
 nt or about CCSRE graduate programming. \n\nYou can also learn more about 
 the PhD Minor program here and browse profiles of current PhD Minors here.
 \n\nWe hope you can join us! Spread the word\; all are welcome.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53992948239143@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T121246Z
DTSTART:20261104T200000Z
DTEND:20261104T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Food Sense: What to Eat and Why
LOCATION:Berg Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/medicine-grand-rounds-with-christophe
 r-gardner-phd
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53992948239143.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/medi
 cine-grand-rounds-with-christopher-gardner-phd\n線上資訊:\nAccess dial
  in details at https://medicine.stanford.edu/education/grand-rounds.html\
 n\nStanford Department of Medicine would like to invite all Stanford commu
 nity members to attend this instance of the Medicine Grand Rounds (MGR) se
 ries\, which will feature Christopher Gardner\, PhD\, a Stanford nutrition
  scientist who has spent three decades rigorously pitting popular diets ag
 ainst each other to find out what actually works\, and whose twin study be
 came the basis of a Netflix docuseries watched by millions.\n\nCE accredit
 ation info here.\n\nCME Activity ID: 58648\n\nMOC Credit (1): Complete thi
 s form after each session\; reflective statement (1-2 sentences) required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53992966927385@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261007T173850Z
DTSTART:20261209T200000Z
DTEND:20261209T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Improbable Research and the Ig Nobel Prizes
LOCATION:Li Ka Shing Center\, Berg Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/medicine-grand-rounds-with-marc-abrah
 ams
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53992966927385.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/medi
 cine-grand-rounds-with-marc-abrahams\n線上資訊:\nAccess dial in detail
 s at https://medicine.stanford.edu/education/grand-rounds.html\n\nStanfor
 d Department of Medicine would like to invite all Stanford community membe
 rs to attend this instance of the Medicine Grand Rounds (MGR) series\, whi
 ch will feature Marc Abrahams\, the founder of the Ig Nobel Prize\, awarde
 d annually to research that "first makes you laugh\, then makes you think\
 ," and the editor of Annals of Improbable Research\, whose career is dedic
 ated to celebrating science at its most surprising and delightfully absurd
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53994605586594@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T121256Z
DTSTART:20261105T023000Z
DTEND:20261105T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with Daniel Lurie and Katerina Ivan
 ov Prado
LOCATION:Building 460\, Margaret Jacks Hall\, Terrace Room (Room 426)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stegner-fellow-reading-with-daniel-lu
 rie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53994605586594.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/steg
 ner-fellow-reading-with-daniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado\n報名/票
 券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSe_0ZHag7NlCSlcVCuDITkqir4iH
 2g6MANsXlu_Md7xQDm6_w/viewform?usp=header\n\nThe Creative Writing Program 
 is pleased to announce a reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry and Fictio
 n: Daniel Lurie and Katerina Ivanov Prado. \n\nThis event is open to Stanf
 ord affiliates and the general public. Registration is encouraged but not 
 required. Register here\n\n____\n\nDaniel Lurie is a Jewish\, rural writer
 \, from eastern Montana. He holds an MFA in Poetry from the University of 
 Idaho and is co-editor of Outskirts Literary Journal. His work has appeare
 d or is forthcoming in Pleiades\, Gulf Coast\, RHINO\, Adroit\, The Iowa R
 eview\, Poetry Northwest\, and other publications. Daniel won the 2026 Mis
 sissippi Review Prize\, Pinch’s Literary Award\, and phoebe’s Greg Gru
 mmer Poetry Contest\, selected by Diane Seuss. He served as the 2025-2026 
 Ronald Wallace Poetry Fellow at the University of Wisconsin-Madison. Find 
 him at danielluriepoetry.com \n\nKaterina Ivanov Prado received an MFA fro
 m the University of Arizona and a Ph.D. in Literature and Creative Writing
  from the University of Houston. Her work has appeared in Narrative\, Brev
 ity\, Catapult\, The Rumpus\, and Joyland\, and has won The Masters Review
  Emerging Fiction Award\, the Narrative Story Contest\, and the AWP Intro 
 Journals Award\, as well as received support from the Elizabeth George Fou
 ndation\, VCCA\, Inprint Houston\, Reese’s Book Club\, and the Bread Loa
 f Writers Conference.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53994806337231@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260917T031408Z
DTSTART:20270128T023000Z
DTEND:20270128T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with Cheyenne Fletcher and Yi Jiang
LOCATION:Building 460\, Margaret Jacks Hall\, Terrace Room (Room 426)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-d
 aniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53994806337231.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/copy
 -of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-daniel-lurie-and-katerina-ivanov-prado\n
 報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdPCA9-27GQk3xyRX0
 XcQCtULFSFTsQeCy2W6ObXVcueJ4QzQ/viewform?usp=header\n\nThe Creative Writin
 g Program is pleased to announce a reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry 
 and Fiction: Cheyenne Fletcher and Yi Jiang. \n\nThis event is open to Sta
 nford affiliates and the general public. Registration is encouraged but no
 t required. Register here\n\n____\n\nCheyenne C. Fletcher (she/they) is a 
 poet\, educator\, auntie\, and citizen of the Cherokee Nation. She is the 
 recipient of Shō Poetry Journal’s Sita Martin Prize and a 2026 Best New
  Poets nominee. She received her MFA from the University of San Francisco.
 \n\nYi Jiang is a writer from Shanghai and Wisconsin. Her fiction has appe
 ared or is forthcoming in Ploughshares\, The Missouri Review\, Gulf Coast\
 , and elsewhere. She completed her MFA at Vanderbilt University\, and has 
 received support from Bread Loaf Writers' Conference. She previously worke
 d as an associate editor at BookPage and a systems engineer in healthcare 
 technology\, prior to a career shift.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53995111265657@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260917T031824Z
DTSTART:20270211T023000Z
DTEND:20270211T040000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stegner Fellow Reading with Priscilla Wathington and Jonat
 hon Atkinson
LOCATION:Building 460\, Margaret Jacks Hall\, Terrace Room (Room 426)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-c
 heyenne-fletcher-and-yi-jiang
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53995111265657.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/copy
 -of-stegner-fellow-reading-with-cheyenne-fletcher-and-yi-jiang\n報名/票
 券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSc9BSvBZUtnyfX6KWy8eXLgjupKc
 ZOMXbi9Hd7K0Omw3iJLBQ/viewform?usp=dialog\n\nThe Creative Writing Program 
 is pleased to announce a reading with Stegner Fellows in Poetry and Fictio
 n: Priscilla Wathington and Jonathon Atkinson. \n\nThis event is open to S
 tanford affiliates and the general public. Registration is encouraged but 
 not required. Register here\n\n____\n\nPriscilla Wathington is a Palestini
 an American poet and the author of the chapbook\, "Paper and Stick". Her p
 oems have appeared in The Adroit Journal\, Four Way Review\, Prairie Schoo
 ner\, Michigan Quarterly Review\, and elsewhere. She was named a Djanikian
  Scholar in 2026 and a Tin House Scholar in 2025\, and has previously serv
 ed as a Sam Mazza Writer in Residence at San Francisco State University’
 s Poetry Center. Wathington is a Senior Poetry Editor at Harbor Review and
  holds an MFA from Warren Wilson College.\n\nJonathon Atkinson is a writer
  from Northern California. His work has appeared in n+1\, New Ohio Review\
 , and Water~Stone Review. He holds an MFA from the University of Minnesota
 \, Twin Cities\, teaches in the Language and Thinking Program at Bard Coll
 ege\, and mentors with the Minnesota Prison Writing Workshop.
END:VEVENT
