 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/marcela-ibanez-cddrl-research-seminar
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54004031461183.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/marc
 ela-ibanez-cddrl-research-seminar\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/
 j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nHow does the local p
 resence of an authoritarian power shape citizens' commitment to democracy?
  Existing theories suggest engagement with authoritarian states should wea
 ken democratic commitment through norm diffusion\, accommodation\, or auth
 oritarian learning. This paper challenges that expectation by examining Ch
 inese development finance in Latin America. Using geocoded data\, it estim
 ates the subnational effect of exposure to Chinese aid on democratic attit
 udes. Exposure increases support for democracy rather than undermining it.
  The effect is strongest in authoritarian and weakly democratic settings a
 nd absent in consolidated democracies. Exposure does not improve perceptio
 ns of China or the United States\, casting doubt on soft power or foreign-
 comparison explanations. Instead\, the paper argues that visible Chinese p
 rojects make incumbents' authoritarian character more salient\, triggering
  an accountability response. These findings recast authoritarian external 
 influence as a contingent catalyst for democratic reinforcement.\n\nABOUT 
 THE SPEAKER\n\nMarcela Ibáñez is a Visiting Postdoctoral Fellow at CDDRL
  and is a Postdoctoral Researcher at the Chair of Political Economy and De
 velopment at the University of Zurich. She holds a Ph.D. in Political Scie
 nce from the University of Mannheim. Her work lies at the intersection of 
 political economy\, development\, and vulnerability reduction. Her researc
 h interests include inequality in low and middle-income economies\, sustai
 nability and de
END:VEVENT
