DTEND:20261112T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Jeanet Sinding Bentzen — Risky Business? Religiosity and
  Risk-Taking in Full-Population Danish Data
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/jeanet-sinding-bentzen-cddrl-research
 -seminar
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54004063149104.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/jean
 et-sinding-bentzen-cddrl-research-seminar\n線上連結: https://stanford.
 zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nWhy do indiv
 iduals differ so persistently in their willingness to take economic risks?
  This paper investigates whether religiosity\, particularly the intensity 
 of religious belief\, and helps explain this variation. The research consi
 ders two competing theoretical predictions: religious teachings discourage
  immoral and unlawful behavior\, while the religious coping literature sug
 gests that belief in a benevolent God reduces background anxiety and effec
 tively provides psychological insurance. Using Danish administrative data 
 covering the full population over three decades\, together with a novel na
 ming-based measure of individual religiosity\, the paper finds that more r
 eligious individuals are significantly more likely to be self-employed and
  to hold a larger share of their assets in stocks. These effects are econo
 mically meaningful\, corresponding to roughly a quarter of the wealth effe
 ct on economic risk-taking. The findings also remain significant when comp
 aring individuals within sibling groups\, which absorbs family-level confo
 unders. The research further finds that these same individuals are less li
 kely to be charged with a criminal offence. This pattern of opposite effec
 ts across different types of risk is consistent with the idea that religio
 sity reduces perceived background risk through a psychological insurance c
 hannel\, while simultaneously increasing the perceived cost of behaviours 
 condemned by religious teachings. Overall\, the paper suggests that religi
 osity helps expla
END:VEVENT
