UID:stanford-54004094461684@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260918T022649Z
DTSTART:20261119T200000Z
DTEND:20261119T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Alain Schläpfer — Why Do Voters Elect Authoritarians? H
 ow Scandal Signals Independence from Elites
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/alain-schlapfer-why-do-voters-elect-a
 uthoritarians-how-scandal-signals-independence-from-elites
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54004094461684.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/alai
 n-schlapfer-why-do-voters-elect-authoritarians-how-scandal-signals-indepen
 dence-from-elites\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pw
 d=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nPoliticians who openly display anti-
 democratic traits can attract substantial electoral support\, even among v
 oters who value democracy. This paper argues that the very behavior that m
 akes a politician objectionable can also reassure voters that the politici
 an will remain independent of political elites. Voters whose preferred pol
 icies lie far from elite interests worry that the candidates they elect wi
 ll be captured by elites once in office. Because capturing a politician mi
 red in scandal is costly for elites\, scandalous behavior can credibly sig
 nal that a candidate is beyond elite reach. Where elites are democraticall
 y committed\, displays of anti-democratic intent become an efficient way t
 o generate such a scandal. Voters may elect these candidates even though t
 hey are more likely to manipulate elections once in office\, an outcome co
 stly for voters and elites alike. Elites who can perfectly anticipate whic
 h politicians will subvert elections have no credible way to communicate t
 his to voters. A reanalysis of existing experimental data is consistent wi
 th the model's central prediction. Unlike existing accounts of anti-democr
 atic voting\, this theory explains why citizens who value democracy may st
 ill elect an anti-democratic candidate\, even when a democratically commit
 ted candidate offers the same policy platform.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nAla
 in Schläpfer is a social science research scholar at Stanford University
 ’s Center on Democracy\, Development and the Rule of Law (CDDRL)\, and a
END:VEVENT
