UID:stanford-54004106866444@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260918T022717Z
DTSTART:20261203T200000Z
DTEND:20261203T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Ivetta Sergeeva — The Authoritarian Passport: How States
  Discipline Emigrants Without Repressing Them
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ivetta-sergeeva-the-authoritarian-pas
 sport-how-states-discipline-emigrants-without-repressing-them
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54004106866444.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/ivet
 ta-sergeeva-the-authoritarian-passport-how-states-discipline-emigrants-wit
 hout-repressing-them\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734
 ?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nCitizenship is conventionally und
 erstood as a source of rights and protection. It is also a source of depen
 dency: states retain distinctive authority over passports\, legal identiti
 es\, civil status\, and mobility that citizens need even when they live ab
 road. Under authoritarianism\, these dependencies become instruments of po
 litical control. The study introduces extraterritorial authoritarian citiz
 enship\, a continuing relationship in which emigrants depend on origin-sta
 te institutions whose provision of nominally universal rights is political
 ly conditional. It generates latent leverage: the capacity to impose costs
  without exercising it against anyone in particular. Emigrants may curb di
 ssent even when never targeted\, and transnational repression activates le
 verage already at work. Using a four-wave panel survey of 4\,000+ Russian 
 emigrants in over 100 countries and 500+ interviews\, the authors show tha
 t what emigrants fear is the open-ended legal and administrative reach of 
 the origin state rather than targeted violence\, that this fear suppresses
  contentious activism specifically\, and that only the protection of anoth
 er state reduces it. (In collaboration with Emil Kamalov and Karolina Nugu
 manova).\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nIvetta Sergeeva specializes in comparativ
 e social science\, focusing on political behavior\, civil society\, citize
 nship and migration. In her research\, she employs a mixed-methods approac
 h\, emphasizing surveys\, statistical modeling\, experiments\, and intervi
 ews. Apar
END:VEVENT
