SUMMARY:[現場] Two Views of the Chinese Coast
LOCATION:David Rumsey Map Center (Green Library)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/two-views-of-the-chinese-coast
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54012791662998.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/two-
 views-of-the-chinese-coast\n報名/票券: https://appointments.library.st
 anford.edu/event/17680622\n\nJoin us for "Two Views of the Chinese Coast\,
 " a talk by Kären Wigen\, the Frances & Charles Field Professor in Stanfo
 rd's Department of History\, at the David Rumsey Map Center. When cartogra
 phic collections began to appear online in the 1990s\, historians of East 
 Asia were quick to seize on maps as subjects for research. Over the next t
 hirty years\, a sophisticated field of critical map studies developed\, il
 luminating the lively landscape of cartographic production across the earl
 y modern Sinosphere. While map-focused research made rapid strides\, a gap
  gradually opened between cartographic research (the history of maps) and 
 what we might call map-informed history (history with maps). \n\nThis talk
  attempts to address that gap by investigating two works from Stanford Uni
 versity Libraries’ collections: a Qing-era scroll painting of the Pacifi
 c coast\, housed in the David Rumsey Map Collection\, and a British map of
  the same shoreline from 1859\, owned by a private collector and held digi
 tally within the Barry Lawrence Ruderman Map Collection. During the 19th c
 entury\, both maps served as tools of military planning\; where the Qing s
 croll was likely consulted for coastal defense\, the British chart was des
 igned for a naval attack. By putting them in dialogue with each other\, th
 e talk suggests how cross-cultural map comparison can help us tell more co
 mplex\, entangled stories about Euro-Asian interaction.\n\nSpeaker Bio\n\n
 Kären Wigen teaches Japanese history and the history of cartography. A ge
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54029201020423@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260920T212556Z
DTSTART:20261115T013000Z
DTEND:20261115T033000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Tracing What Remains: Memory\, Place\, and Chinese America
 n Histories
LOCATION:Anderson Collection\, Denning Family Resource Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/tracing-what-remains-memory-place-and
 -chinese-american-histories
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54029201020423.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/trac
 ing-what-remains-memory-place-and-chinese-american-histories\n報名/票
 券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSf8K9K_f55rlureRm8AbFnJ8YkMQ
 sXCDllfpsdkWjyLMuhYAg/viewform?usp=preview\n\nPresented in conjunction wit
 h Patty Chang's surfacing\, Tracing What Remains: Memory\, Place\, and Chi
 nese American Histories brings together films that examine the histories o
 f Chinese communities across Northern California through landscape\, memor
 y\, and acts of remembrance. Moving between documentary\, historical narra
 tive\, and poetic reflection\, these films trace the enduring presence of 
 communities whose lives and labor were systematically erased from the hist
 orical record.\n\nFrom the displaced Chinese fishing villages of the San F
 rancisco Bay to the expulsion of Chinese residents from Eureka\, these wor
 ks reveal how places can hold memory even when official histories do not. 
 Together\, they ask how artists can recover forgotten stories\, map absenc
 es\, and imagine new ways of bearing witness to the past.\n\nFollowing the
  screening\, filmmakers\, Chisa Hughes (Many Moons) and Rene Yung (Chinese
  Whispers) will join in conversation with surfacing co-curator\, Kelsey Ch
 en to discuss filmmaking as a practice of historical recovery\, the relati
 onship between place and memory\, and the ongoing work of preserving Chine
 se American histories.\n\nRSVP HERE\n\nFilms\n\nMany Moons \n\n2023 | 21:4
 3 minutes\n\nIn the county which once boasted it was “free of Chinamen\,
 ” Chinese community members search for those who may have survived Humbo
 ldt's 1885 expulsion and 60 years of enforced exclusion– the only known 
 survivor being Charlie Moon\, whose descendants are Native tribal members.
  Framed by the words of a ghost
END:VEVENT
