SUMMARY:[現場] Electric Contrasts Study Evening
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/electric-contrasts-study-evening
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54032052037077.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/elec
 tric-contrasts-study-evening\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/Kzn8W8MrWvz
 j3L2y7\n\nElectric Contrasts: The Art of the Detail brings together photob
 ooks and magazines to show how the revolution of photography in the late 1
 9th and early 20th century changed the history of art. Electric Contrasts 
 takes Kenneth Clark’s landmark 1938 book One Hundred Details from Pictur
 es in the National Gallery as a starting point to illuminate the impact bl
 ack and white photographic details of Old Master paintings had on new ways
  of seeing in the early 20th century. The Bowes Art and Architecture Libra
 ry exhibition reveals the anachronistic and provocative juxtapositions of 
 artworks in avant-garde magazines and books which created a radically new 
 vision that would go on to influence television and the internet.\n\nThis 
 Study Evening brings together Geraldine Johnson and Alexander Nemerov to d
 iscuss the role of the detail in art history\, moderated by the exhibition
 ’s curator\, Altair Brandon-Salmon.\n\n6:00pm: Tour of the Electric Cont
 rasts exhibition in Bowes Art and Architecture Library with Altair Brandon
 -Salmon6:30pm: Introduction to the Study Evening with Altair Brandon-Salmo
 n\, Oshman Hall6:40pm: Geraldine Johnson on Medardo Rosso7:00pm: Alexander
  Nemerov on André Malraux’s The Voices of Silence7:20pm: Audience Q&AGe
 raldine Johnson is Associate Professor in the History of Art at the Univer
 sity of Oxford. She will discuss the Italian sculptor-photographer Medardo
  Rosso (1858-1928) and his deployment of details from installation shots t
 aken at the 1904 Salon d'Automne in Paris in
END:VEVENT
