SUMMARY:[現場] Kurtis Schaeffer: "Poetry in Tibet: Pushing Boundaries of 
 Buddhist Language and Thought"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/kurtis-schaeffer-poetry-in-tibet-push
 ing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-thought
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54036622167796.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/kurt
 is-schaeffer-poetry-in-tibet-pushing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-t
 hought\n\nAbstract:\n\nTibetan writers of kāvya-style poetry exploited th
 e structured flexibility of the medium to praise the heroes and ideals of 
 Buddhist tradition\, to innovate in both literary form and practices of th
 ought\, and at times even to transgress the strictures implicit in other g
 enres of Buddhist literature. This talk explores the popular genre of sing
 le verses intended to illustrate the poetic features described in Daṇḍ
 in's Mirror of Poetry\, and suggests that kāvya poetry was an important s
 pace in elite literature where pre-modern Tibetan writers could openly pus
 h the boundaries of Buddhist language\, thought\, and feeling.\n\n \n\nBio
 :\n\nKurtis Schaeffer is a student of Buddhist literature in Tibet and the
  Himalayas. He is the translator of The Life of the Buddha (2014) and Budd
 hist Meditation: Classic Teachings from Tibet (2024)\, both from Penguin C
 lassics\, and the author of Himalayan Hermitess (2004)\, The Culture of th
 e Book in Tibet (2009)\, and other books. He is the Frances Myers Ball Pro
 fessor in the Department of Religious Studies at the University of Virgini
 a.
END:VEVENT
