UID:stanford-54037411908366@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T164734Z
DTSTART:20261119T200000Z
DTEND:20261119T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Jannis Panagiotidis- Racism against East Europeans in Germ
 any: History and Present
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, William J. Perry Conference Room\, Encina Hall Centr
 al\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/racism-against-east-europeans-in-germ
 any-history-and-present
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54037411908366.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/raci
 sm-against-east-europeans-in-germany-history-and-present\n線上連結: ht
 tps://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_mwn-XbggT_KUsVxkMnQ0xg\n報名/
 票券: https://luma.com/3d1y7ndc\n\nRacism against East Europeans constit
 utes a major gap in racism research and anti-racism debates. Yet there is 
 a long tradition of treating the region and its inhabitants as inferior\, 
 with devastating consequences throughout history. This book is the first c
 omprehensive study of racism in the entangled histories of Germany and Eas
 tern Europe—a racism that is not based on skin color and affects people 
 despite their mostly phenotypical whiteness. Embedded in colonial imaginat
 ions of Eastern Europe\, anti-East European racism took its most radical a
 nd brutal shape during Nazism. But racist knowledge persisted across the r
 upture of 1945 and the Cold War and took on new shapes after the fall of c
 ommunism in 1989\, when East-West migration once again became an important
  feature of European and German reality. This book makes a case for the lo
 ng overdue eastward expansion of the contemporary racism debate. \n\n \n\n
 Jannis Panagiotidis is a historian and migration scholar. He is scientific
  director of the Research Center for the History of Transformations (RECET
 ) at the University of Vienna. His publications include the monographs The
  Unchosen Ones: Diaspora\, Nation\, and Migration in Israel and Germany (I
 ndiana UP 2019)\, Postsowjetische Migration in Deutschland: eine Einführu
 ng (Beltz Juventa 2021)\, and Antiosteuropäischer Rassismus in Deutschlan
 d: Geschichte und Gegenwart (Beltz Juventa 2024\, with Hans-Christian Pete
 rsen).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54037437866563@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260922T013755Z
DTSTART:20261203T200000Z
DTEND:20261203T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Jana Hunter- The Child of Freedom: Temporal Sovereignty in
  Habsburg Bohemia
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, William J. Perry Conference Room\, Encina Hall Centr
 al\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-child-of-freedom-temporal-soverei
 gnty-in-habsburg-bohemia
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54037437866563.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 child-of-freedom-temporal-sovereignty-in-habsburg-bohemia\n線上連結: h
 ttps://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_mwn-XbggT_KUsVxkMnQ0xg\n報名
 /票券: https://luma.com/pd3ig2ef\n\nWho decides what time it is? In Habs
 burg Bohemia\, that question drew in nearly every other: nation and empire
 \, identity and sovereignty\, capital and a newly connected world. Railway
 s and telegraphs created a time shared across distance\, and with it a deb
 ate over who had the power to set it. Histories of time standardization ha
 ve told a story of unification\; histories of Habsburg sovereignty\, meanw
 hile\, have located its conflicts in diets and parliaments. This talk brin
 gs them together\, asking how an empire tried to synchronize every local c
 lock\, where authority over time was located\, and why time became a quest
 ion of sovereignty. After 1867\, Bohemia's unresolved constitutional statu
 s moved into the infrastructure of daily life: whose observatory produced 
 noon\, which meridian ran the trains\, whose clocks kept the time. At stak
 e was not only who ruled Bohemia\, but how time could be controlled at all
 . \n\nBy the turn of the century\, Bohemians called their divided time dua
 lismus\, the word for the constitutional settlement itself. This talk trac
 es these jurisdictional disputes over four decades from railway decrees to
  universal time. The lines of conflict did not run simply between empire a
 nd nation: German liberals\, Czech nationalists\, intellectuals\, and busi
 nessmen appealed to historic rights\, national identity\, commercial inter
 est\, and civic embarrassment. Jurisdiction over time was scalar\, dispers
 ed across ministries\, railway companies\, international conferences\, and
  municipal c
END:VEVENT
