 lexis-madrigal
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54045658779121.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/what
 -is-a-public-intellectual-today-alexis-madrigal\n報名/票券: https://st
 anforduniversity.qualtrics.com/jfe/form/SV_cHIH2DzsPVcgrm6\n\nPlease join 
 us for the autumn event of the Stanford Public Humanities flagship speaker
  series\, What Is a Public Intellectual Today\, which will feature journal
 ist Alexis Madrigal. He will be in conversation with Adrian Daub on craft\
 , career\, and culture in the Bay Area and beyond. Book signing and sellin
 g to follow for both authors. \n\nPlease RSVP at this link to attend the e
 vent. \n\nAlexis Madrigal is an acclaimed multimedia journalist in Oakland
 \, CA. He is the co-host of KQED’s current affairs show Forum\, creator 
 of the 8-part podcast Containers\, author of the newsletter The Oakland Ga
 rden Club\, and a contributing writer at The Atlantic\, where he co-founde
 d The COVID Tracking Project. His book The Pacific Circuit explores how a 
 logistical revolution that began in Oakland has reshaped American cities. 
 He is the proprietor of Local Economy\, a community space in Oakland\, and
  has been a visiting scholar at UC Berkeley’s School of Information and 
 the Center for Science\, Technology\, Medicine & Society. \n\nAdrian Daub 
 is J.E. Wallace Sterling Professor in the Humanities at Stanford Universit
 y\, where he serves as the Faculty Director of the Clayman Institute for G
 ender Research. He is the author of What Tech Calls Thinking (2020) and Th
 e Cancel Culture Panic (2024)\, and writes for numerous US and European ne
 wspapers and magazines.\n\nThis event is cosponsored by the Department of 
 Communication\, the Journalism Program\, the Michelle R. Clayman Institute
  for Gender Research\, the Stanford Of
END:VEVENT
