SUMMARY:[現場] Daniel Wurgaft | A Resource Rational Analysis of Represent
 ations: Geometry\, Inference\, Capacity
LOCATION:Stanford Neurosciences Building\, James Lin and Nisa Leung Seminar
  Room\, E153
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-daniel-wurgaft
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54047808526631.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds
 -seminar-daniel-wurgaft\n\nAbstract\n\nNeural systems process information 
 by forming representations of the world. Studies of representational geome
 try have revealed remarkably structured representations in biological and 
 artificial systems\, as well as surprising alignment in representations ac
 ross systems. Why do representations across systems converge to similar st
 ructures\, how do these structures encode concepts and uncertainty over th
 em\, and how are they shaped by resource constraints?\n\nHere\, we tackle 
 these questions in neural networks through the lens of resource rationalit
 y\, asking what representations are optimal for solving a task\, given dim
 ensionality and linear readout constraints. We derive a notion of optimal 
 linear representations\, and show that these emerge across varied architec
 tures and tasks\, providing a simple explanation for representational conv
 ergence as alignment toward the optimal solution. This shared treatment mo
 tivates a normative formalization of conceptual spaces that generalizes th
 e linear representation hypothesis. We then extend this account to uncerta
 inty and inference over conceptual spaces\, asking how representations sho
 uld change in response to in-context evidence\, and finally to resource-co
 nstrained settings involving representational interference.\n\n\nAbout the
  Center for Neural Data Science Seminar Series \n\nThe Center for Neural D
 ata Science Seminar Series is a platform for trainees across campus to sha
 re  insights and innovative approaches that bridge the gap between
END:VEVENT
