UID:stanford-54047926358295@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261001T203932Z
DTSTART:20261215T000000Z
DTEND:20261215T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] YoungJu Jo | Data-driven discovery and control of neural c
 omputations
LOCATION:Stanford Neurosciences Building\, James Lin and Nisa Leung Seminar
  Room\, E153
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-youngju-jo
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54047926358295.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds
 -seminar-youngju-jo\n\nAbstract\n\nThe brain is a nonlinear dynamical syst
 em whose evolving population activity gives rise to computations that guid
 e behavior. We will present a data-driven systems neuroscience framework t
 hat integrates large-scale neurophysiology\, interpretable dynamical syste
 ms modeling\, and targeted perturbations in cognitive animal models. This 
 approach has enabled the discovery of cell-type-specific and brain-wide dy
 namical structures underlying reward and memory computations\, including a
 ttractor manifolds that integrate past rewards and steer future decision-m
 aking. To enable causal tests and model-guided control of these computatio
 ns\, we have developed optical and genetic technologies for cellular-resol
 ution\, bidirectional control of neural populations\, along with optimal c
 ontrol approaches for designing perturbations. Together\, these converging
  advances set the stage for a closed-loop strategy to measure\, model\, an
 d control neural dynamics and computations\, toward the long-term vision o
 f writing complex information into the brain.\n\nBio \n\nYoungJu Jo is an 
 Assistant Professor and the Brody Family Faculty Fellow in the Department 
 of Electrical Engineering and Computer Sciences and the Helen Wills Neuros
 cience Institute at the University of California\, Berkeley. His laborator
 y develops and applies next-generation neurotechnologies to measure\, mode
 l\, and control neural dynamics and computations in behaving animals. He c
 ompleted his postdoctoral training in Bioengineering in 2026\, 
END:VEVENT
