SUMMARY:[現場] What Do We Owe Each Other in the Age of AI?
LOCATION:Paul Brest Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/what-do-we-owe-each-other-in-the-age-
 of-ai
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54054961140361.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/what
 -do-we-owe-each-other-in-the-age-of-ai\n報名/票券: https://bit.ly/arro
 w27\n\nPlease note that this event is in-person only\, and RSVPs are reque
 sted to attend. Walk-ins are welcome.\n\nRegister now!\n\nThe 2026-27 Arro
 w Lecture features Betsey Stevenson discussing transformative AI\, policy\
 , and a vision for shared societal progress.\n\nArtificial intelligence pr
 omises to make us richer. But will it make our lives better? At this event
 \, economist Betsey Stevenson will draw on economic history and research o
 n happiness and meaning to demonstrate why greater productivity does not a
 utomatically translate into human flourishing. She will explore how we can
  turn AI’s promise into shared progress by pacing job transformation\, r
 eturning shares of AI-generated wealth to the public\, and strengthening s
 ources of meaning beyond paid employment. The goal\, Stevenson will argue\
 , is not to preserve every job or reject technological change but to build
  a society in which our worth as human beings does not depend on outperfor
 ming a machine.\n\nSpeakers:\n\n💡 Betsey Stevenson is a professor of ec
 onomics and public policy at the University of Michigan. She is a member o
 f the Board of Directors of Lyft\, Inc and a faculty research associate at
  the National Bureau of Economic Research\, a fellow of the Ifo Institute 
 for Economic Research in Munich\, and an elected member of the National Ac
 ademy of Social Insurance. Previously\, she served as a member of the Bide
 n-Harris transition team at the U.S. Treasury\, a member of President Obam
 a’s Council of Economic Advisers and the Chief Economis
END:VEVENT
