SUMMARY:[現場] Bones\, Borders\, and Black Mirrors: Can Justice Outlive C
 olonialism?
LOCATION:Alumni Center\, Fisher Conference Center: Lane/Lyons/Lodato
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/bones-borders-and-black-mirrors-can-j
 ustice-outlive-colonialism
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54098980339839.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/bone
 s-borders-and-black-mirrors-can-justice-outlive-colonialism\n報名/票券
 : https://cvent.me/eLOL30\n\nJoin IAJS for Bones\, Borders\, and Black Mir
 rors: Can Justice Outlive Colonialism? as we consider how colonialism cont
 inues to shape our world and explore where\, in the law\, in the archaeolo
 gical record\, and in art\, we are finding ways to build more just societi
 es.\n\nInspiring this conversation is artwork by Pedro Lasch\, Coatlicue &
  Las Meninas: The Stanford Edition\, originally commissioned as part of a 
 national program co-hosted by Stanford’s Institute for Advancing Just So
 cieties and Zócalo Public Square\, and now installed at the Institute. In
  conversation with artist and professor\, Pedro Lasch (Duke University)\, 
 are international legal scholar Tendayi Achiume (Stanford Law School)\, an
 d archeology professor Krish Seetah (Doerr School of Sustainability)\, mod
 erated by sociologist and IAJS Faculty Director\, Tomás Jiménez (Sociolo
 gy).\n\nIdeas Mosaic is an Institute for Advancing Just Societies quarterl
 y series where scholars\, practitioners\, and artists converge to shape bo
 ld ideas for a more just world.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54099709560880@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260929T182510Z
DTSTART:20270205T000000Z
DTEND:20270205T043000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Shosan Victoria Austin: "Zen Work Practice: Cultivating a 
 Field of Blessings"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/zen-work-practice-cultivating-a-field
 -of-blessings
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54099709560880.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/zen-
 work-practice-cultivating-a-field-of-blessings\n\nWhat can ancient work pr
 actices offer Silicon Valley today? Hidden in more than a thousand years o
 f Sōtō Zen monastic codes\, we can trace the development of what we migh
 t now call a 90-day serious role-playing game. Its purpose: to spiritually
  mature practitioners\, separately and together\, for their own benefit an
 d that of all beings.\n\nIn this lecture\, we will explore aspects of Chin
 ese\, Japanese\, and Western monastic work codes to find out how they prom
 ote human-hearted qualities such as generosity\, morality\, forbearance\, 
 and energy. Familiarity with these practices can help us illuminate the me
 aning and function of our responsibilities to ourselves\, our work groups\
 , our communities\, and our world.\n\n4:00pm - Guided Meditation (Room TBD
 )\n\n7:00pm - Lecture (East Asia Library\, Room 224)\n\nBio:\n\nShosan Vic
 toria Austin began practicing Zen in 1971. She trained in the U.S.\, in In
 dia\, and in Japan\, and is entrusted as a Dharma heir in the lineage of S
 hunryu Suzuki Roshi (1999)\, an international Soto Zen priest (2005)\, and
  a Dharma teacher at San Francisco Zen Center (1999). Some of her previous
  San Francisco Zen Center roles include Work Leader\, Tenzo\, Tanto\, and 
 President. In 2022\, she co-led the training and participation of the Ryob
 an (ceremonial assembly) for the Zenshuji 100th Anniversary Jukai-e in Los
  Angeles\, a five-day retreat in which over 100 people received precept te
 achings and vows.\n\nIn addition to her deep study and daily teaching of y
 ogic aspects of meditation—in div
END:VEVENT
