SUMMARY:[現場] Unsettling the New AI Status Quo
LOCATION:Tresidder Union\, Cypress Lounge\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/unsettling-the-new-ai-status-quo
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54110219615265.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/unse
 ttling-the-new-ai-status-quo\n報名/票券: https://ethicsinsociety.stanf
 ord.edu/events/unsettling-new-ai-status-quo\n\nJoin this thought-provoking
  showcase on the practical implementation of ethics developed by the 2026 
 Ethics and Technology Practitioner Fellows.\n\nThe Ethics and Technology P
 ractitioner Fellows have dedicated the past year to exploring and addressi
 ng critical issues at the intersection of technology and ethics through in
 quiry\, stakeholder engagement\, and pushing for reforms in organizations 
 and fields of practice. Projects are in areas ranging from community delib
 eration on data centers\, to AI-accelerated carbon emissions\, to regulati
 ng AI slop. We welcome anyone invested in the future of technology\, inclu
 ding those in civil society\, policymaking\, industry\, and academia to co
 me join the discussion of how we exercise leadership in a space filled wit
 h barriers and complexities working against change.\n\nSee the full showca
 se schedule.\n\nThis event will have a photographer present to document th
 e event. No personal recordings (audio or visual) are allowed. By attendin
 g\, you consent for your image to be used for Stanford-related promotions 
 and materials. If you have any questions\, please contact ethics-center@st
 anford.edu.\n\nIf you require disability-related accommodation\, please co
 ntact disability.access@stanford.edu as soon as possible or at least 7 bus
 iness days in advance of the event.\n\nSee more information about visitor 
 parking. \n\nLearn more about the McCoy Family Center for Ethics in Societ
 y.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54112511034966@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261028T231657Z
DTSTART:20261113T003000Z
DTEND:20261113T020000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Making Letters: Typography Across Hand\, Screen\, and Loom
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/making-letters-typography-across-hand
 -screen-and-loom
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54112511034966.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/maki
 ng-letters-typography-across-hand-screen-and-loom\n\nRSVP Link: HTTPS://TI
 NYURL.COM/STSAMY26\n\nHow do the tools used to create letterforms shape th
 e way those forms are imagined\, constructed\, and understood? In this pre
 sentation\, graphic designer\, educator\, type designer\, and fiber artist
  Amy Copeland traces her evolving relationship with typography across anal
 og\, digital\, and textile practices. Beginning with hand-lettered high sc
 hool book covers and the construction of letterforms with gouache and a ru
 ling pen in college\, she follows this trajectory through professional art
  direction and digital typesetting\, original typeface design\, and her re
 cent project of weaving the alphabet.\n\nThrough this practice-based accou
 nt\, Copeland considers how typographic knowledge moves between technologi
 es and materials. Digital tools make letterforms reproducible\, scalable\,
  and precisely adjustable\; weaving translates those same forms into physi
 cal structures governed by warp\, weft\, tension\, sequence\, and touch. T
 hese material constraints do more than alter the appearance of a letter—
 they influence how it is conceived and constructed.Rather than treating an
 alog and digital processes as opposites\, Copeland explores the productive
  exchange between them. Her work demonstrates how knowledge gained through
  one form of making can migrate into another\, revealing letters not as fi
 xed symbols but as adaptable structures shaped by tools\, materials\, and 
 embodied experience.\n\n----------\n\nAmy Copeland loves to make things. A
  graphic designer and educator f
END:VEVENT
