UID:stanford-54124995976202@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261103T020604Z
DTSTART:20261118T200000Z
DTEND:20261118T213000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Greek and Arabic Reception of Ptolemy's Almagest betwe
 en the 7th and 9th Centuries | Maria Mavroudi
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ptolemys-almagest
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54124995976202.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/ptol
 emys-almagest\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSff
 d2KijnqGN3E334Z5Fqh8TiOsYLtGjmXtfssGV8jcerexbQ/viewform?usp=header\n\nThe 
 period between the seventh and the ninth centuries has frequently been por
 trayed in scholarship as “dark”\, especially because its intellectual 
 production appears low. Overall\, it is deemed as an empty interval betwee
 n the end of the intellectual efflorescence of antiquity and the presumed 
 renaissance of philosophy and the sciences in the Arab world enabled by th
 e Greek-to-Arabic translation movement in ninth- and tenth-century Baghdad
 . This talk will re-examine these scholarly assessments by using the recep
 tion of Ptolemy’s Almagest as an example.\n\n\n\nMaria Mavroudi was born
  in Thessaloniki\, Greece and studied Philology at the University of her n
 ative city before earning a Ph.D. in Byzantine studies at Harvard. Her sch
 olarly work begun by focusing on a tenth-century Byzantine book on dream i
 nterpretation that had been widely received in Latin and the European vern
 aculars and counted as the Christian dreambook of the Middle Ages. While g
 enerally viewed as a Byzantine invention partly based on the second-centur
 y manual of Artemidorus\, she showed that it was a Christian adaptation of
  Arabic Islamic material and one among a larger group of texts originally 
 written in Arabic or Persian and received into Greek between the ninth and
  the fifteenth centuries. During the next two decades\, she worked on iden
 tifying the place of these translations within Byzantine literary culture 
 and its reception in “East” and “West’ during the medieval and ear
 ly modern period. This begs reconsideri
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54125978094433@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T121901Z
DTSTART:20261103T233000Z
DTEND:20261104T004500Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Book Talk — We Shall Outlast Them: Putin’s Global Camp
 aign to Defeat the West
LOCATION:Encina Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-we-shall-outlast-them-putin
 s-global-campaign-to-defeat-the-west
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54125978094433.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/book
 -talk-we-shall-outlast-them-putins-global-campaign-to-defeat-the-west\n線
 上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/93963090864?pwd=i2uxiraWsDRoO7K3iYQZ
 MkZFxalZmM.1\n報名/票券: https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/book-ta
 lk-we-shall-outlast-them-putins-global-campaign-defeat-west\n\nHow has Rus
 sia sustained its confrontation with the West since 2022\, and at what cos
 t? In her new book\, Hanna Notte argues the Kremlin is waging a war not on
 ly on Ukraine but over the global order itself.\n\nNotte traces how Russia
  adapted after the invasion. It worked around sanctions\, built new partne
 rships\, cultivated support across the Global South and tried to deepen di
 visions within Western democracies. Her account combines policy analysis w
 ith field reporting from more than a dozen countries and interviews with o
 fficials\, facilitators and ordinary people drawn into Russia's agenda.\n\
 nThe book also examines the domestic consequences. Notte finds that Putin'
 s focus on confronting the West has distorted Russia's economy and moved t
 he country from a tacit dictatorship toward a full one. She suggests Putin
  may outlast the West\, but has gambled Russia's future in the process.\n\
 nFSI Satre Family Senior Fellow and Mosbacher Director of CDDRL\, Kathryn 
 Stoner will moderate the discussion\, which will cover Russian statecraft\
 , the durability of autocratic regimes under pressure\, and what Russia's 
 strategy means for the U.S.\, Europe\, and the Global South. Audience Q&A 
 will follow.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nHanna Notte is the Director of the Eu
 rasia Nonproliferation Program at the James Martin Center for Nonprolifera
 tion Studies. She is also a senior associate (non-resident) with the Cente
 r for Strategic and International Studies and with the Kennan Institute. N
 otte's expertise is in Russia's
END:VEVENT
