SUMMARY:[線上] Shop Smart\, Eat Well: Budget-Friendly Grocery Tips\, Meal
  Ideas and Label-Reading Strategies
LOCATION:https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#shop-smart
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/shop-smart-eat-well-budget-friendly-g
 rocery-tips-meal-ideas-and-label-reading-strategies
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54136301350931.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/shop
 -smart-eat-well-budget-friendly-grocery-tips-meal-ideas-and-label-reading-
 strategies\n線上連結: https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-even
 ts.html#shop-smart\n報名/票券: https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lect
 ures-events.html#shop-smart\n\nErin Hodgson\, MS\, RD\, BC-ADM\, CDCES\, o
 ffers practical strategies to stretch a grocery budget while building diab
 etes-friendly meals. She will share how to read nutrition labels to compar
 e nutrition information\, identify added sugars and hidden sodium\, and pl
 an cost-effective meal ideas that support steady glycemic control. \n\nThi
 s presentation is part of the monthly Diabetes Wellness Group webinar seri
 es from the Stanford Medicine Diabetes Care Program. Webinars are free and
  open to adults with diabetes and their families. You don’t need to be a
  Stanford patient to join.\n\nThis webinar will be presented by Erin Hodgs
 on\, MS\, RD\, BC-ADM\, CDCES\, who is a clinical dietitian and certified 
 diabetes care and education specialist at Stanford Health Care.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54136475283456@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T121927Z
DTSTART:20261111T003000Z
DTEND:20261111T020000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] A Rift in Time. Reinhart Koselleck and the Theory of Histo
 ry
LOCATION:Building 200\, History Corner\, 307
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/a-rift-in-time-reinhart-koselleck-and
 -the-theory-of-history
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54136475283456.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/a-ri
 ft-in-time-reinhart-koselleck-and-the-theory-of-history\n\nWhat possible f
 utures does history contain? Reinhart Koselleck has become one the most wi
 dely read and cited twentieth-century German historians and theorists of h
 istory\, not just for the new field of “global conceptual history” but
  essentially for anyone across disciplines writing about questions of time
 \, temporality\, and historical thought. Yet the recent uptick of interest
  in his work as part of the “temporal turn” obscures the fact that Kos
 elleck was working throughout his life on something even more ambitious: a
  new theory of history or rather a theory of the conditions of possible hi
 stories\, and hence possible futures\, written by an historian in an age o
 f catastrophes.\n\nBased on a wealth of previously inaccessible archival m
 aterials\, this talk explores Koselleck’s critique of the modern concept
  of History and what he called its “temporalization”—from Critique a
 nd Crisis to his essays on dreams and war memorials—and parses out his p
 arallel search for an antidote in the form of a new theory of historical k
 nowledge\, most visible in his late essay collection Sediments of Time. We
  will ask what remains of Koselleck’s theoretical reckoning with rupture
 d time and repetition in history and whether it can be untangled from the 
 experiences of loss and complicity of his generation.\n\nJoin the Modern E
 urope Workshop in learning more about and discussing Stefan-Ludgwig Hoffma
 n's research. RSVP for refreshments.\n\nStefan-Ludwig Hoffmann is Chancell
 or’s Professor of History at the Unive
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54161263754671@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261005T181541Z
DTSTART:20261113T000000Z
DTEND:20261113T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Delivering Humanitarian Aid in a Contested World
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, Bechtel Conference Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/delivering-humanitarian-aid-in-a-cont
 ested-world
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54161263754671.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/deli
 vering-humanitarian-aid-in-a-contested-world\n報名/票券: https://app.s
 martsheet.com/b/form/01a0872afab87e4fb63479be86ac5f29\n\nJoin us for a two
 -part panel discussion with IsraAID and partners exploring the future of h
 umanitarian assistance in an increasingly contested and conflict-ridden wo
 rld. The first panel will examine how aid organizations deliver relief whe
 n access\, neutrality\, and safety are absent or precarious\, drawing on I
 sraAID's experience responding to crises across the globe. The second pane
 l will turn to the Middle East\, focusing on the promise and practical cha
 llenges of Arab-Israeli cooperation in delivering humanitarian assistance 
 to communities in need around the region.\n\nAGENDA\n3:30 - 4:00 PM: GATHE
 RING AND REGISTRATION\n\n4:00 - 4:10 PM: INTRODUCTION\n\nSpeaker: Larry Di
 amond (Mosbacher Senior Fellow in Global Democracy\, Freeman Spogli Instit
 ute for International Studies\, Stanford University)\n\n4:10 - 4:50 PM: PA
 NEL ONE — Delivering Humanitarian Assistance in 2026: Challenges and Opp
 ortunities\n\nSonali Korde (Senior Advisor\, All Hands and Hearts)Raphael 
 Marcus (Deputy CEO\, IsraAID)Moderator: David Cornfield (Anne T. and Rober
 t M. Bass Professor of Pediatric Pulmonary Medicine\, Stanford University)
 \n\n4:55 - 6:00 PM: PANEL TWO — Lessons from the Middle East: Arab-Israe
 li Cooperation in Humanitarian Aid\n\nDr. Yaroup Ajlouni (President\, Jord
 an Health Aid Society International)Diana Shehade-Nama (Director of Fundra
 ising\, Marketing and Communications\, Rozana–Health Diplomacy)Yotam Pol
 izer (Global CEO\, IsraAID)Dr. Khaula Sawah (President\, Union of Medical 
 Care and Relief Organizations-USA)Mode
END:VEVENT
