BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54170450699649@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T202429Z
DTSTART:20261106T170000Z
DTEND:20261107T003000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Big Tech\, Democracy\, and Human Rights
LOCATION:Alumni Center\, Fisher Conference Room ( Barnes/McDowell/Cranston)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/big-tech-democracy-and-human-rights
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54170450699649.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/big-
 tech-democracy-and-human-rights\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/uGhKMyy1
 MWLvuJqu9\n\nThis conference is hosted by the Center for South Asia (Stanf
 ord Global Studies)\, with the Center for the Study of Organized Hate (CSO
 H)\, We Are Noor\, and DRF USA.\n\nAbout the conference\nA handful of tech
 nology companies now shape how billions of people communicate and take par
 t in public life\, and most of those people live in the Global Majority: A
 frica\, Latin America\, the Caribbean\, and parts of Asia and Oceania. The
  day asks why rights and safety protections stop at the borders of a few r
 ich countries\, and what it would take for them to reach the people who ne
 ed them most. Three panels and four conversations move from ideology (the 
 tech right)\, to geopolitics (AI governance)\, to remedies (online harms a
 nd accountability)\, to the labor and money that sustain counter-power.\n\
 nPanels and Firesides\nPanel 1: The Tech Right and the Global Majority : I
 deology\, Design\, and the Politics of Inevitability\nFireside: What Silic
 on Valley's Authoritarian Embrace Means for the Global Majority\nPanel 2: 
 AI Governance and the Geopolitics of Emerging Technology\nPanel 3: Online 
 Harms and Accountability: Why protection Stops at the Borders\nFireside: W
 ho Pays for Accountability? Funding Digital Rights After the Cuts\n\nMore 
 details about the Big Tech\, Democracy\, and Human Rights Conference can b
 e found here.
END:VEVENT
