BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54177973515463@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T022421Z
DTSTART:20261106T013000Z
DTEND:20261106T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Queer Thing about Sin | Harry Tanner
LOCATION:Humanities Center\, Levinthal Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-queer-thing-about-sin-harry-tanne
 r
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54177973515463.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 queer-thing-about-sin-harry-tanner\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com
 /forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeqJgPkBi9yPAjkLWSQtvv2htLXjua34iEHDDVcdTvseuBqCA/viewfo
 rm\n\nIn The Queer Thing About Sin (Bloomsbury 2025)\, Harry Tanner reveal
 s the ancient origins of homophobia. By the end of the 5th century BCE\, G
 reek plays were being staged which ridiculed homosexuality\, by the end of
  the next century Athenians were being tried for homosexuality and Plato h
 ad explicitly condemned it in his Laws. In Rome\, the legal situation was 
 hardly better and in the law codes of Leviticus same-sex sex is condemned.
  Why did this come to happen\, and what warnings does it hold for us now? 
 \n\nTanner will explore some of his findings in this talk and then turn hi
 s attention to how to write and research academic research for a trade aud
 ience — he will argue that trade publishers have unfairly received a rep
 utation for simplifying research\, and his method for going from a PhD to 
 a book proposal and an internationally published trade book. His talk will
  address the need to adapt scholarly writing and research and work with Ar
 tificial Intelligence to build new\, more human insights. He will also tal
 k briefly about his own experience of conversion therapy and how it shaped
  his research in Classics. \n\nHarry Tanner earned a PhD in Classics at th
 e University of Galway\, Ireland (2023). He has published on Greek Linguis
 tics\, specifically on semantic change in Ancient Greek\, appeared on Siri
 usXM and a variety of podcasts including The Guilty Feminist\, and written
  for The New York Sun. His book The Queer Thing About Sin was shortlisted 
 for Book of the Year in the UK by Foyles. H
END:VEVENT
