SUMMARY:[混合] The Rise and Fall of Tunisian Democracy: Populism\, Polari
 zation\, and the Perils of Power-Sharing
LOCATION:Encina Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-rise-and-fall-of-tunisian-democra
 cy-populism-polarization-and-the-perils-of-power-sharing
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54188103812302.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 rise-and-fall-of-tunisian-democracy-populism-polarization-and-the-perils-o
 f-power-sharing\n線上資訊:\nRegister for Zoom link: https://app.smart
 sheet.com/b/form/01a0fef6e22471b3873d96fd3493adf7\n報名/票券: https://
 app.smartsheet.com/b/form/01a0fef6e22471b3873d96fd3493adf7\n\nAfter the Ar
 ab Spring\, Tunisia emerged as the one success story to transition to demo
 cracy\, winning a Nobel Peace Prize in 2015 for its focus on inclusion and
  power-sharing. How\, then\, did its democracy collapse in 2021?\n\nDrawin
 g on unique interviews with senior Tunisian officials\, alongside three na
 tionally representative surveys of the population\, The Rise and Fall of T
 unisian Democracy points to an overemphasis on compromise\, consensus\, an
 d power-sharing. Although power-sharing institutions can be useful at the 
 start of a transition\, the book finds that extending them beyond that poi
 nt can lead democracy to unravel by frustrating voters and spurring the ri
 se of populists and extremists. The book takes an in-depth look at Tunisia
 's transition and then explores how far the theory travels through a quant
 itative analysis of all democratic transitions between 1942–2020. Overal
 l\, the book reveals the dark side of power-sharing\, broadening our under
 standing of the causes of populism\, polarization\, and democratic breakdo
 wn.\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nSharan Grewal (PhD\, Princeton 2018) is an Ass
 ociate Professor of Government at American University and a Nonresident Se
 nior Fellow at the Brookings Institution. His research examines revolution
 s and democratic transitions\, particularly in the Arab world. His first b
 ook\, Soldiers of Democracy? Military Legacies and the Arab Spring (Oxford
  University Press\, 2023)\, explores why militaries support or thwart demo
 cratic transitions. It received AP
END:VEVENT
