SUMMARY:[現場] “Don’t Eat the Mermaid: Exploring the Legend of Yaobik
 uni”
LOCATION:Humanities Center\, Levinthal Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/dont-eat-the-mermaid-exploring-the-le
 gend-of-yaobikuni
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54199804558288.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/dont
 -eat-the-mermaid-exploring-the-legend-of-yaobikuni\n\nScreening of a recen
 t documentary on Yaobikuni followed by a conversation with Michael Dylan F
 oster and Hank Glassman\n\nDescription:\n\nThe legend of Yaobikuni tells o
 f a young woman who inadvertently eats the flesh of a ningyo (mermaid) and
  is therefore blessed—or cursed—with immortality and eternal youth. Ou
 tliving her family and friends\, she eventually abandons society to travel
  throughout Japan as a nun. Finally\, after eight hundred years she return
 s to her birthplace\, current day Obama City in Fukui Prefecture\, to ente
 r into eternal prayer in a local cave. \n\nFor this presentation we will f
 irst screen a recent television documentary (28 mins) that explores how Ya
 obikuni is understood by current residents in Obama. This will be followed
  by a conversation about the legend itself\, the broader Buddhist context 
 surrounding it\, and the making of the documentary. \n\nParticipants: \n\n
   Michael Dylan Foster (University of California\, Davis) \n\n  Hank Glass
 man (Haverford College) \n\n \n\nBios: \n\nMichael Dylan Foster is Profess
 or in the Department of East Asian Languages and Cultures at the Universit
 y of California\, Davis. He is the author of Pandemonium and Parade: Japan
 ese Monsters and the Culture of Yōkai (University of California Press 200
 9)\, The Book of Yōkai (University of California Press 2015\; 2024)\, and
  numerous articles on folklore\, monsters\, and media. His edited volumes 
 include The Folkloresque: Reframing Folklore in a Popular Culture World (U
 tah State University Press 2015)\, Mat
END:VEVENT
