SUMMARY:[線上] Stanford Medicine Neurosurgery Master Class: Motion Preser
 vation of the Spine
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stanford-medicine-neurosurgery-master
 -class-motion-preservation-of-the-spine
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54301361744065.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/stan
 ford-medicine-neurosurgery-master-class-motion-preservation-of-the-spine\n
 報名/票券: https://stanford.cloud-cme.com/course/courseoverview?P=0&EI
 D=60418\n\nOverviewThis activity will educate physicians on motion-preserv
 ing spinal surgery\, including cervical and lumbar disc arthroplasty\, as 
 an alternative to fusion. Participants will learn to identify appropriate 
 surgical candidates\, understand contemporary techniques and technologies 
 used in complex\, multilevel\, and revision spinal reconstructions\, and a
 pply current evidence to patient counseling and clinical decision-making.\
 n\nPresenter: Anand Veeravagu\, MD\, Professor of Neurosurgery\, Stanford 
 Univeristy\n\nRegistrationRegistration for all practitioners - free.\n\nTo
  register for this activity\, please click HERE\n\nCreditsAMA PRA Category
  1 Credits™ (1.00 hours)\, Non-Physician Participation Credit (1.00 hour
 s)\n\nTarget AudienceSpecialties - Internal Medicine\, Primary Care & Popu
 lation Health\, Sports MedicineProfessions - Non-Physician\, Physician Obj
 ectivesAt the conclusion of this activity\, learners should be able to:\n1
 . Describe the indications and patient selection criteria for motion-prese
 rving spinal surgery (e.g.\, cervical and lumbar disc arthroplasty) versus
  fusion-based approaches\n2. Identify the surgical techniques and technolo
 gies used in complex spinal reconstructions\, including multilevel and rev
 ision cases\n3. Evaluate strategies for avoiding and managing complication
 s associated with motion preservation and complex reconstructive procedure
 s\n4. Apply current evidence and long-term outcomes data to guide decision
 -making in complex spine cases requiring reconstruction\n\nAccredi
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54302138029167@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261021T162518Z
DTSTART:20261210T013000Z
DTEND:20261210T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Print Revolutions: Chinese Impressions\, 1630s–1960s\, w
 ith Clarissa von Spee (Curator\, Cleveland Museum of Art)
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/print-revolutions-chinese-impressions
 -1630s1960s-with-clarissa-von-spee-curator-cleveland-museum-of-art
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54302138029167.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/prin
 t-revolutions-chinese-impressions-1630s1960s-with-clarissa-von-spee-curato
 r-cleveland-museum-of-art\n\nIn August 2025\, the Cleveland Museum of Art 
 (CMA) and the Metropolitan Museum of Art (the Met) announced the acquisiti
 on of well over 200 rare\, 18th century Suzhou woodblock prints from the c
 ollection of Christer von de Burg. The purchase brings some of the most so
 phisticated Chinese color prints to the United States\, enabling both inst
 itutions to tell the story of printmaking\, in combination with their worl
 d-class painting collections\, in new ways and innovative displays.\n\nThe
  talk will introduce the exhibition\, Print Revolutions: Chinese Impressio
 ns\, 1630s–1960s at the CMA  (15 November\, 2026 - 4 April\, 2027)  show
 casing some of the finest exemplars of Chinese printmaking\, many of them 
 seen in public for the first time. More than eighty prints\, paintings\, a
 nd porcelains from the museum’s collection\, as well as a few loans\, il
 lustrate the development of color printing from the 1600s leading up to th
 e international modern woodcut movement in the early 1900s.  \n\nClarissa 
 von Spee\, PhD\, is Chair of the Department of Asian Art and James and Don
 na Reid Curator of Chinese Art\, as well as Interim Curator of Islamic Art
  at the Cleveland Museum of Art. \n\nImage: Gusu Beauty–Mother and Son i
 n a Summer Pavilion (姑蘇美人 – 夏日亭畔母子圖)\, 1736–95. 
 Southeast China\, Suzhou Qing dynasty (1644–1911)\, Qianlong period (173
 6–95). Polychrome woodblock print\; ink and color on paper\; print only:
  104.2 x 54.5 cm (41 x 21 7/16 in.)\; overall: 112.9 x 62.9 cm (44 7/16 x 
 24 3/4 in.). The Cleveland Museum of Art
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54304055437613@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261021T212503Z
DTSTART:20261202T200000Z
DTEND:20261202T210000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Pediatric Pulmonology in the Primary Care Setting
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/pediatric-pulmonology-in-the-primary-
 care-setting
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54304055437613.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/pedi
 atric-pulmonology-in-the-primary-care-setting\n報名/票券: https://stan
 ford.cloud-cme.com/course/courseoverview?P=5&EID=60362\n\nOverviewPediatri
 c primary care providers play an important role in diagnosing and managing
  children and young adults with pulmonary conditions. In this webinar\, pr
 imary care providers will learn more about difficult to diagnose and treat
  pulmonary conditions that they may see in their practice. These could inc
 lude severe or atypical asthma\, recurrent or persistent pneumonia\, sleep
  disorders (account for about 40% of all pulmonary referrals)\, abnormal c
 hest radiographs\, and chronic cough. Providers will leave this webinar wi
 th the ability to diagnose and provide daily management of these condition
 s and learn also when to consider consulting with a pediatric pulmonary sp
 ecialist for advice.\n\nRegistrationRegistration for all practitioners - f
 ree\n\nTo register for this activity\, please click HERE.\n\nCreditsAMA PR
 A Category 1 Credits™ (1.00 hours)\, AAPA Category 1 CME credits (1.00 h
 ours)\, ANCC Contact Hours (1.00 hours)\, Non-Physician Participation Cred
 it (1.00 hours)\n\nTarget AudienceSpecialties - PediatricsProfessions - Ad
 vance Practice Nurse (APN)\, Non-Physician\, Nurse\, Physician\, Physician
  Associate ObjectivesAt the conclusion of this activity\, learners should 
 be able to:\n1. Identify common patient and caregiver education needs rela
 ted to the management of pediatric asthma\n2. Discuss how to proceed when 
 a patient has an unclear asthma diagnosis or difficult-to-treat asthma\n3.
  Identify how to treat patients with recurrent pneumonia\n4. Define how to
  approach treating chronic cough
END:VEVENT
