DTSTAMP:20261102T205428Z
DTSTART:20261104T200000Z
DTEND:20261104T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Lunch Club Series | Navigating Heritage: Chinook Art and P
 ublic History Along the Columbia River
LOCATION:Building 500\, Archaeology Center\, 106
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/lunch-club-series-3721
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54321714796042.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/lunc
 h-club-series-3721\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/registe
 r/WN_-XB5tRt_TACr_ppYR5oBvQ\n\nLunch Club provides affiliates of the Stanf
 ord Archaeology Center with a community-oriented forum for engagement with
  current issues in archaeology. On Nov 4\, 2026\, we will host Dr.Jon Daeh
 nke from University of California\, Santa Cruz.\n\nAbstract: \n\nIn this t
 alk I explore the contested nature of marking Indigenous history and conti
 nued presence/absence in settler colonial states. Specifically\, I look at
  monuments\, memorials\, and other sites of public history and heritage al
 ong the Columbia River that offer reminders of Indigenous – and in this 
 case\, specifically Chinook – occupation of the landscape. In part\, thi
 s work addresses the challenges that the Chinook Indian Nation faces regar
 ding the public representation of their own history\, especially at “off
 icial” sites of heritage. But centrally in the work I explore how the Ch
 inook are responding to\, and actively shaping\, discussions and practice 
 surrounding monuments\, memorials\, and sites of heritage. In part this is
  through increased participation in management of “official” sites of 
 heritage. But it is also connected to the role that the revitalization of 
 Chinookan art has played in reclaiming heritage landscapes\, as well as op
 portunities for the Chinook to tell their own stories on their own lands.
END:VEVENT
