DTSTAMP:20261023T215459Z
DTSTART:20270224T200000Z
DTEND:20270224T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Distinguished Lecture Series Lunch Talk | From Plans to Pe
 ople: Rough-and-Ready Reconstructions of a Bronze Age Citadel
LOCATION:Building 500\, Archaeology Center\, 106
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/distinguished-lecture-series-lunch-ta
 lk-from-plans-to-people-rough-and-ready-reconstructions-of-a-bronze-age-ci
 tadel
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54321924632772.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/dist
 inguished-lecture-series-lunch-talk-from-plans-to-people-rough-and-ready-r
 econstructions-of-a-bronze-age-citadel\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoo
 m.us/webinar/register/WN_O5L3NyY1RbezMITLQsAL6w\n\nEach quarter\, the Stan
 ford Archaeology Center invites prominent archaeologists from around the g
 lobe to be in residence for a week as a Distinguished Lecturer. During the
 ir residency\, the Distinguished Lecturer gives two lectures and interacts
  with faculty\, postdoctoral scholars and students. Stanford Archaeology C
 enter will host Prof. Christopher H. Roosevelt from  Koc University over t
 wo days (Feb 24 and Feb 25 for the Winter Quarter of this academic year. \
 n\n \n\nAbstract:\n \n\nVisual and numeric reconstructions of archaeologic
 al sites can become their most evocative and indelible portraits. A bird's
 -eye view or a population figure often conveys a palpable sense of an anci
 ent community and outlives the data used to produce it. Because such recon
 structions are so easily convincing and lasting\, their resolution should 
 match that of their underlying datasets. Yet the history of archaeology\, 
 from Çatalhöyük to Troy and beyond\, is rife with disputed reconstructi
 ons and corrected population estimates. We should question not how accurat
 e such reconstructions must be\, but what resolution the evidence can hone
 stly support.\n\nUsing recent work of the Kaymakçı Archaeological Projec
 t in western Anatolia\, this lecture presents a combined approach to visua
 l and numerical reconstructions of a second-millennium BCE citadel and its
  population that privileges the spatial and chronological resolution of it
 s datasets over the photorealism or exactitude of its outputs. Aerial and 
 ground-based remote
END:VEVENT
