UID:stanford-54322087931985@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261026T155124Z
DTSTART:20270226T010000Z
DTEND:20270226T023000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Distinguished Lecture Series| From Disentanglement to Re-E
 ntanglement: Integrating the Living Archaeological Record
LOCATION:Building 500\, Archaeology Center\, 106
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/distinguished-lecture-series
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54322087931985.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/dist
 inguished-lecture-series\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/r
 egister/WN_Euv4s6fIRyiB3heE7WKV0Q\n\nEach quarter\, the Stanford Archaeolo
 gy Center invites prominent archaeologists from around the globe to be in 
 residence for a week as a Distinguished Lecturer. During their residency\,
  the Distinguished Lecturer gives two lectures and interacts with faculty\
 , postdoctoral scholars and students. Stanford Archaeology Center will hos
 t Prof. Christopher H. Roosevelt from  Koc University over two days (Feb 2
 4 and Feb 25 for the Winter Quarter of this academic year. \n\nAbstract:\n
  \n\nRecent trends in archaeological data management have focused on prese
 rving\, linking\, comparing\, and reusing datasets\, and rightly so. The e
 xpensive work of archaeological practice is too often relegated to inacces
 sible files and legacy databases\, recoverable only when future opportunit
 ies afford revived engagement. While legacy datasets are one clear challen
 ge\, so too are living datasets\, those still growing\, still under analys
 is. As archaeological practice is increasingly specialized\, its domain-sp
 ecific archives have increasing difficulty speaking to one another. Cerami
 cs\, small finds\, botanical and faunal remains\, conservation records\, d
 rawings\, photographs\, and spatial data accumulate in parallel systems th
 at share an archaeological site but can otherwise become siloed. This happ
 ens to born-digital projects too. The Kaymakçı Archaeological Project (K
 AP) was born digital in 2014\; by 2026 its records were accessed across se
 venteen separate database forms\, on their way to legacy status.\n\nThis l
 ecture prese
END:VEVENT
