DTSTAMP:20261024T005836Z
DTSTART:20270120T200000Z
DTEND:20270120T210000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] The Spiritual Highway: Religious World Making in Megacity 
 Lagos - Professor Marloes Janson
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-spiritual-highway-religious-world
 -making-in-megacity-lagos-professor-marloes-janson
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54323097577907.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 spiritual-highway-religious-world-making-in-megacity-lagos-professor-marlo
 es-janson\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/H5CFNgAxdF3wekwE6\n\nSession D
 escription:\n\nOpened in 1978 at the height of Nigeria’s oil boom\, the 
 Lagos-Ibadan Expressway was once celebrated as a symbol of progress. Howev
 er\, rising traffic volumes and decades of neglect gradually transformed i
 t into one of the country’s deadliest roads\, earning it the nickname 
 “Highway of Death”. Yet even as it declined as a transport corridor\, 
 it flourished as a stage for public religiosity. Today\, lined with vast p
 rayer camps\, it is widely known as the “Spiritual Highway”. \n\nIn th
 is panel discussion\, photographer Dr Akintunde Akinleye and anthropologis
 t Professor Marloes Janson will explore how religious organizations have e
 merged as influential city-builders in Lagos\, Nigeria’s economic hub. D
 rawing on ethnographic research and photography\, they will show how the L
 agos-Ibadan Expressway has become a key site for understanding the relatio
 nship between religion and urban development. Along this corridor\, religi
 on is not simply a feature of urban life\; it is a constitutive force thro
 ugh which the city itself is made and remade.\n\nSpeaker Biography:\n\nMar
 loes Janson is Professor of West African Anthropology at SOAS University o
 f London. She holds a PhD in Anthropology from Leiden University\, The Net
 herlands. Her publications include Islam\, Youth\, and Modernity in the Ga
 mbia: The Tablighi Jamaʻat (Cambridge University Press\, 2014)\, which ha
 s been awarded the RAI Amaury Talbot Prize for African Anthropology\; Cros
 sing Religious Boundaries: Islam\, Christianity\, and ‘
END:VEVENT
