DTSTAMP:20261026T155609Z
DTSTART:20270129T013000Z
DTEND:20270129T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Claudia Hart: Techno-ethics and the Sociology of Simulatio
 n
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/claudia-hart-techno-ethics-and-the-so
 ciology-of-simulation
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54346327748253.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/clau
 dia-hart-techno-ethics-and-the-sociology-of-simulation\n報名/票券: htt
 ps://sls-claudia-hart.eventbrite.com\n\nI was early into simulations - whi
 ch isolated me because I found myself inside one of the most male-dominate
 d spaces in contemporary culture: the world of simulation technologies. It
 s denizens were mostly ex-military\, its technologies built to visualize w
 ar games. When I started in 1990\, its culture seemed devoid of women. My 
 response was to build a feminist practice inside of that militarized space
 . My practice is trans-media and spans 3D animation\, virtual and augmente
 d reality\, performance\, illustrated books\, and installation. I appropri
 ate Western canonical figures — philosophers\, poets\, architects\, pain
 ters — and reinvent their dense and sober languages as theatrical\, ofte
 n dark fantasy worlds. But the rooms I build echo with women’s voices: v
 oices that I also perform\, script\, and digitally transform. I am mutable
  and so are computer files — a perfect fit! My themes are serious (techn
 o-ethics\, the sociology of simulation\, the politics of embodiment)\, alt
 hough my methodology is theatrical and allegorical.\n\nOriginally trained 
 in art and architectural history at New York University and Columbia Unive
 rsity\, Claudia Hart began her career in the New York City art scene befor
 e transitioning to digital media in the late 1990s. Her historical trainin
 g remains foundational to her work. Rather than treating technology as det
 ached from the past\, Hart situates digital simulation within a long conti
 nuum of representation extending from Renaissance perspective and photogra
 phy to virtua
END:VEVENT
