 CRMA) Stage
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/sound-voice-strings-compositions-for-
 traditional-east-asian-instruments
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54348724664796.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/soun
 d-voice-strings-compositions-for-traditional-east-asian-instruments\n\nAn 
 evening of new music for stringed traditional East Asian instruments. Feat
 uring Katherine Whatley (koto and voice)\, Southeast of Rain (pipa\, voice
 \, electronics)\, and Stella Da Yun Song (haegeum).\n\nAbout the performer
 s and pieces:\n\nKatherine Whatley\n\nKatherine Whatley is a koto musician
 \, composer\, and PhD Candidate in East Asian Languages and Cultures at St
 anford University. She plays both traditional and experimental koto\, both
  improvised and composed. Her compositions have been performed by the Stan
 ford New Ensemble and the Earplay Ensemble. She has performed in a variety
  of contexts including at the Good Shepherd Center (Seattle)\, Columbia Un
 iversity (New York)\, on tour across Japan and in Berlin.\n\nHer dissertat
 ion analyzes music and soundscape in medieval Japanese literature. Her res
 earch has previously been published in The February Journal\, positions\, 
 Japanese Journal of Religious Studies\, and Migrating Minds. As a journali
 st\, she has worked with the BBC\, Dwell\, Conde Nast\, New York Times\, t
 he Japan Times\, and others.\n\n今、箏ひびき(Ima Koto Hibiki)—Now\
 , Here Koto Sings\n\nThis piece for 13-string koto and voice is based on R
 yōjin Hisho\, a 12th-century collection of imayō (popular songs). Ryōji
 n Hisho draws from songs taught to the emperor by Otomae\, a courtesan. Be
 cause no original scores remain\, this composition starts from this point 
 of unknown to have a conversation across time. The piece is designed to en
 courage improvisation\, with modular components that can be played in
END:VEVENT
