SUMMARY:[線上] Team Science in Child Health Research: When the Whole is G
 reater than the Sum of the Parts with Dr. Mary Leonard
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/meeting/register/uWnqvBKATe6zgzKXo5vY_w#/
 registration
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/team-science-in-child-health-research
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54355994709896.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/team
 -science-in-child-health-research\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/
 meeting/register/uWnqvBKATe6zgzKXo5vY_w#/registration\n報名/票券: http
 s://stanford.zoom.us/meeting/register/uWnqvBKATe6zgzKXo5vY_w#/registration
 \n\nThe Laurie J. Girand Center for Biomedical Ethics is pleased to announ
 ce Dr. Mary Leonard as our Team Science Tuesday speaker for the Fall Quart
 er for a presentation titled “Team Science in Child Health Research: Whe
 n the Whole is Greater than the Sum of the Parts.” We invite you to join
  us on Zoom on Tuesday\, November 10\, 2026 at 11:00am. \n\nMary B. Leonar
 d\, MD\, MSCE is the Arline and Pete Harman Professor and Chair\, Departme
 nt of Pediatrics at Stanford University\, Director of the Stanford Materna
 l and Child Health Research Institute (MCHRI)\, and Physician-in-Chief of 
 Lucile Packard Children’s Hospital. Her research examines how chronic di
 seases—including kidney disease\, inflammatory bowel disease\, rheumatol
 ogic conditions\, and childhood cancer—impair bone and muscle developmen
 t across the lifespan. Answering these questions requires partnership with
  colleagues in multiple pediatric subspecialties\, as well as in biostatis
 tics\, epidemiology\, anthropology\, physics\, engineering\, and bioengine
 ering\, whose expertise underpins the advanced imaging and analytic method
 s central to her work. This collaborative model has allowed her to transla
 te mechanistic findings into clinical trials and population-level studies 
 while training investigators from many disciplines in patient-oriented res
 earch.\n\nIf you have any questions\, please reach out to Nicole Foti (nfo
 ti@stanford.edu) or Daphne Martschenko (daphnem@stanford.edu) .\n\nRegiste
 r now.
END:VEVENT
