DTSTART:20261204T020000Z
DTEND:20261204T033000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Forms & Frequencies | Xina Xurner with Tzotzona
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center\, Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/forms-frequencies-xina-xurner-with-tz
 otzona
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54365069773602.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/form
 s-frequencies-xina-xurner-with-tzotzona\n報名/票券: https://thethirdpl
 ace.is/event/xina-xurner\n\nDoors at 6pm. Performances start at 6:30pm.\n\
 nCantor Arts Center presents Forms & Frequencies\, a Thursday night music 
 series featuring musicians and sound artists from the Bay Area and beyond.
 \n\nJoin us on Thursday\, December 3 for a night of pounding electronics a
 nd unruly physicality with Xina Xurner and Tzotzona\, with visuals by arti
 st Christopher Richmond.\n\nXina Xurner is a Los Angeles-based sadical and
  sexperimental music and performance duo with vocalist\, sculptor\, and pe
 rformance artist Young Joon Kwak and musician and producer Marvin Astorga.
  Fusing industrial noise\, power electronics\, dark club-wrecking beats\, 
 electric guitar\, and extreme vocal distortion\, their body-shaking live s
 hows unleash queer trans-femme rage\, refusal\, and joy.\n\nCreating an at
 mosphere that feels part club\, part collective exorcism\, Xina Xurner’s
  performance will be paired with visuals by artist Christopher Richmond\, 
 whose alien and terrestrial landscapes juxtapose reality and illusion\, us
 ing handmade props\, special photography\, and varied drawing styles to pr
 esent multiple pictorial universes in constant kinetic interaction.\n\nTzo
 tzona\, Astorga’s solo project\, will open the evening with brown-futuri
 st electronic music that synthesizes elements of Cumbia\, Technobanda\, ho
 use\, techno\, acid\, Quebradita\, and 3bal. Taking inspiration and sample
 s from Astorga’s upbringing in the El Paso-Ciudad Juárez Borderplex\, a
  pre-Columbian imaginary\, and 90s-era Mexican television\, Tzotzona looks
  to the recent and a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54366133070873@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261029T224103Z
DTSTART:20261116T200000Z
DTEND:20261116T213000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Book Talk: Nuremberg's Citizen Prosecutor
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, 030 Garden Level
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-nurembergs-citizen-prosecut
 or
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54366133070873.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/book
 -talk-nurembergs-citizen-prosecutor\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.co
 m/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSchWE97EVewUajclN2yb6o2_Xdtu0rAvBOrNbqcTZZxElgTrw/viewf
 orm?usp=dialog\n\nJoin the Center for Human Rights and International Justi
 ce as Professor Gregory Gordon presents his new book\, which chronicles th
 e remarkable life of Benjamin Ferencz. Ferencz is known as one of the twen
 tieth century’s great warriors for justice\, from Nuremberg to the first
  trial of the International Criminal Court. \n\nAbout the author: \n\nGreg
 ory S. Gordon is a Professor of Law at Peking University School of Transna
 tional Law. Not long after earning undergraduate and law degrees from Berk
 eley\, he served as an attorney at the International Criminal Tribunal for
  Rwanda on the landmark “Media” cases\, the first international post-N
 uremberg incitement prosecutions of media executives\, earning a commendat
 ion from U.S. Attorney General Janet Reno. He subsequently worked with the
  U.S. Department of Justice (DOJ)\, serving\, in sequence\, as a street cr
 ime\, white collar crime\, organized crime and then human rights prosecuto
 r (the latter for the Office of Special Investigations\, the so-called “
 Nazi Hunters Unit”). He has tried 24 cases\, 23 to a favorable verdict. 
 Professor Gordon was detailed by DOJ to Sierra Leone to conduct a post-civ
 il war justice assessment and served as a Special Assistant U.S. Attorney 
 for the District of Columbia. From 2006 to 2014\, he was a professor at UN
 D Law and served as Professor Gordon is the author of Atrocity Speech Law:
  Foundation\, Fragmentation\, Fruition (Oxford University Press\, 2017) an
 d his new monograph Nuremberg’s Citizen Prosecutor: Benjamin Ferencz
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54366374879644@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261028T222447Z
DTSTART:20261113T003000Z
DTEND:20261113T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] The Idea that Made U.S.: Dialogues on Freedom: 25 Years Af
 ter 9/11: The History of Surveillance\, Technology\, and Democracy
LOCATION:Traitel Building\, Hauck Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-idea-that-made-us-dialogues-on-fr
 eedom-25-years-after-911-the-history-of-surveillance-technology-and-democr
 acy
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54366374879644.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 idea-that-made-us-dialogues-on-freedom-25-years-after-911-the-history-of-s
 urveillance-technology-and-democracy\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/UpQ
 tqY2hNobxxcxU8\n\nA quarter century after 9/11\, advances in surveillance 
 and technology have transformed how the United States protects its securit
 y\, and raised enduring questions about privacy\, government power\, and d
 emocratic accountability.\n\nFormer FBI Director Christopher Wray and form
 er Deputy National Security Advisor for Cyber and Emerging Technology Anne
  Neuberger join Hoover Institution senior fellow Amy Zegart to examine how
  the security landscape has changed since 9/11\, how emerging technologies
  are reshaping it once again\, and how a free society can navigate the cha
 llenges ahead.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54366529796320@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261028T225431Z
DTSTART:20270107T200000Z
DTEND:20270107T210000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Lunchtime Curator Talk | Solid Pictures
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center\, Pigott Family Gallery (142)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-lunchtime-curator-talk-solid-
 pictures-9909
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54366529796320.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/copy
 -of-lunchtime-curator-talk-solid-pictures-9909\n報名/票券: https://the
 thirdplace.is/event/sp\n\n​Join Patrick R. Crowley\, Associate Curator o
 f European Art\, on this special highlights tour of Solid Pictures.\n\nBef
 ore 3D printing\, there was photosculpture. Curated by Patrick R. Crowley\
 , Associate Curator of European Art\, Solid Pictures is the first major ex
 hibition to explore these largely overlooked objects first conceived in 18
 59 by the twenty-nine-year-old French artist and inventor François Willè
 me. Employing projection technologies and mechanical instruments\, Willèm
 e and his team of operators translated photographic negatives into solid f
 igures across a variety of media\, aiming to create affordable portrait sc
 ulpture for the burgeoning middle class of Second Empire France. Though co
 mmercially unsuccessful\, these experiments laid the groundwork for the sc
 anning\, machining\, and printing technologies ubiquitous today. Bringing 
 together these rare artifacts\, the exhibition opens up a little-known cha
 pter of history to reveal the roots and richness of our contemporary world
 . An accompanying catalogue features four newly commissioned essays accomp
 anied by previously unpublished documentation\, deepening our understandin
 g of this overlooked chapter in the history of art and technology.\n\nRSVP
  here.\n\n________\n\nParking\n\nPaid visitor parking is available along L
 omita Drive as well as on the first floor of the Roth Way Garage Structure
 \, located at the corner of Campus Drive West and Roth Way at 345 Campus D
 rive\, Stanford\, CA 94305. From the Palo Alto Caltrain station\, the Cant
 or 
END:VEVENT
