URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/bechtel-international-center-resource
 -fair
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54383678300700.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/bech
 tel-international-center-resource-fair\n\nJoin us at the Bechtel Internati
 onal Center as we officially kick off International Education Week!\n\nCam
 pus partners\, student groups\, cultural tables\, and performances will be
  the highlights with special snacks. Please join us as we celebrate global
  education and cultural exchange.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54400058337177@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261103T014131Z
DTSTART:20261118T004500Z
DTEND:20261118T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Malês:  Film Screening and Conversation
LOCATION:Bolivar House\, Conference Room
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/males-film-screening-and-conversation
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54400058337177.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/male
 s-film-screening-and-conversation\n\nThis event is a collaboration between
  the Consulate General of Brazil in San Francisco and Stanford University\
 , through its Program on International and Cross-Cultural Education\, Glob
 al Studies Division\, Language Center\, Abbasi Program in Islamic Studies\
 , Center for African Studies\, Department of Iberian and Latin American Cu
 ltures\, and Center for Latin American Studies.\n\nScreening of Antônio P
 itanga's 2024 historical film telling the homonymous 1835 uprising of ensl
 aved Muslim workers in Salvador\, Bahia\, Brazil.\n\nA couple about to get
  married is kidnapped from their homeland in Africa and brought to Brazil 
 as slaves. Separated by fate\, they both struggle to survive the horrors o
 f slavery and become involved in the largest Brazilian slave uprising.\n\n
 Hors d'oeuvre and conversation will follow the screening.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54409686628220@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261102T195601Z
DTSTART:20261111T010000Z
DTEND:20261111T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Faculty Candidate Talk: “Candyman 5x: Playing Wretched i
 n the Candyman Films of Bernard Rose and Nia DaCosta” | Zayla Crocker\, 
 PhD Candidate\, UNC-Chapel Hill
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/faculty-candidate-talk-candyman-5x-pl
 aying-wretched-in-the-candyman-films-of-bernard-rose-and-nia-dacosta-zayla
 -crocker-phd-candidate-unc-chapel-hill
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54409686628220.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/facu
 lty-candidate-talk-candyman-5x-playing-wretched-in-the-candyman-films-of-b
 ernard-rose-and-nia-dacosta-zayla-crocker-phd-candidate-unc-chapel-hill\n\
 nSituated in Black media studies and play studies\, Zayla Crocker examines
  Bernard Rose’s 1992 film Candyman along with Nia DaCosta’s remake of 
 the same name to demonstrate how both films encourage the spectator to “
 play” within the genre conventions of the slasher film through an act of
  ritualized play Crocker deems “playing wretched.” Both films overtly 
 or covertly prompt the spectator to call on and summon the performance of 
 Black memory\, history\, and trauma in the figure of a hook-handed slasher
  villain. As a whole\, Crocker argues that the affective performance of ho
 rror and play acts as a memorial vehicle that situates the memory of Black
  trauma within the lived reality of the spectator. Outside of the film\, s
 pectators can join the game\, and they too can call on Candyman and summon
  the memory of the other.\n\nZayla Crocker is a PhD Candidate and teaching
  fellow in the English Department at the University of North Carolina at C
 hapel Hill. Her academic research focuses on the liberatory potential of t
 he horror genre with an emphasis on media theory and cultural trauma throu
 gh novels\, films\, and video games. Currently\, her research is centered 
 on Black expressions of trauma in the horror genre with a cross-media expl
 oration of memory and affect.\n\nVISITOR INFORMATION\nOshman Hall is locat
 ed in the McMurtry Building on Stanford campus at 355 Roth Way. Visitor pa
 rking is free after 4pm on weekdays\, except by the Oval. Alternatively\, 
 take the Caltrain to Palo Alto Transit Center and
END:VEVENT
