SUMMARY:[現場] German Studies Lecture Series: Jutta Eming - How Do You Da
 nce a Cabinet of Curiosities?
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, Rm 216
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/german-studies-lecture-series-jutta-e
 ming-how-do-you-dance-a-cabinet-of-curiosities
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54411448205345.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/germ
 an-studies-lecture-series-jutta-eming-how-do-you-dance-a-cabinet-of-curios
 ities\n\nPlease join German Studies Lecture Series for How Do You Dance a 
 Cabinet of Curiosities? A conversation with Professor Jutta Eming (Freie U
 niversität Berlin).\n\nAs a collection and exhibition convention\, a Cabi
 net of Curiosity or Wunderkammer is regarded as a historical intermediate 
 stage between the medieval treasury and the modern museum. It was widespre
 ad throughout Europe from the 16th to 18th century until its holdings bega
 n to be distributed to newly founded natural history\, art\, or ethnologic
 al museums in the 18th and 19th century. Taking a recent choreography as a
  starting point\, this talk introduces the view that the Wunderkammer is n
 ot primarily an early modern form of collection\, but a cultural-historica
 l phenomenon that spans epochs and is part of a continuum of discourses on
  the marvelous. This includes the fact that the Wunderkammer has obviously
  become a broader cultural studies topic and forms a reference for a varie
 ty of recent academic and non-academic trends. In premodern times these ca
 binets constitute a European view of the world with curiosity in “monstr
 ous” races on its margins\, and a practice of perception in which narrat
 ion\, appropriation\, collecting\, and presentation are closely linked. In
  recent times this interest in curiosity has flipped in emancipatory ways:
  The Wunderkammer now seems to provide a space in which humans negotiate t
 he limits of binary identity and redefine their place in the world.\n\nRSV
 P for the Jutta Eming event here.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
